WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:09.063
- This is the 62nd annual Hume Lecture.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.360
It's a great prestigious event each year.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.300
The annual lecture in honor
of Dr. Edward H. Hume,

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.370
which is made possible by the generosity

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.440
of his family and many friends.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.020
As you may know, Dr. Hume
devoted his long career

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:23.970
to working in China and elsewhere

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:27.210
in the cause of healthcare
and medical training.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.480
He graduated from Yale
College back in 1897

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.520
and received a medical
degree four years later

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.590
from Johns Hopkins.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.150
Dr. Hume worked in India from 1903 to 1905

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.230
before going to China,

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.600
where he founded the
Shanghai School of Medicine

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.850
and the Shanghai Hospital,

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.310
previously known as the
Shanghai Medical School.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:51.480
Under the auspices of Yale
China Association in Changsha,

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.000
Dr. Hume also served as the president

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.810
of the Colleges of Yale
in China in the 1920s.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.220
He returned to the United States,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.560
but was recalled back to China in '34

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.860
and worked for several years
in health administration

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.970
in liaison with the Chinese
National Health Administration.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.250
From 1937 until his retirement,

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.050
he directed the Christian Medical Council

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:15.570
for overseas work in New York City.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.330
Shortly after his death in 1957,

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:21.390
funds from colleagues, friends, and family

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.670
enabled the establishment
of this lectureship

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:28.530
to bring to Yale eminent
scholars of East Asian studies.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.900
And we have one such
eminent scholar here today

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:34.230
whom we're very pleased
and delighted to welcome.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:37.410
Professor Nicolas Standaert
is professor of Sinolgy

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.220
at KU Leuven in Belgium

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.760
and a member of the Royal Academy

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.980
of Sciences and Arts of Belgium.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:48.000
He took his PhD from
Leiden University in 1984,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.710
and has spent the years since then

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:53.040
teaching mainly in Flemish
and Dutch speaking countries

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.983
with the occasional foray
into China and North America.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.810
Professor Standaert has
produced a decade of monographs,

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.720
numerous co-written or co-edited volumes,

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.420
and he's made great
contributions to the field, also,

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.270
with volumes like "The Handbook
of Christianity in China."

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.850
Those two thick volumes that sit

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.040
in all of our research libraries.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:17.550
His own major research
focus is cultural contacts

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:22.080
between China and Europe in the
17th and the 18th centuries.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.070
His work is on the history
of books, people, and ideas

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:29.370
that crossed between China and Europe.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.890
He writes and reads extraordinarily well

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.440
on both sides of that divide.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:38.070
So a work like his "Chinese
Voices in the Rites Controversy"

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:41.250
analyzes the Chinese end of
a story that we often hear

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.230
from European perspectives.

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:44.227
While his latest book,

00:02:44.227 --> 00:02:46.620
"The Chinese Gazette in European Sources:

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.900
Join in the Global Public in the Early

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.490
and Mid-Qing Dynasty,"

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:53.040
looks from the opposite
end of the viewfinder

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.603
at European reception.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.170
The arc of his work
began with Yang Tingyun,

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.050
thinking about his influence on...

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.510
The influence of Confucian
and Buddhist ideas

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.080
on Christian thinking in that
period in the 17th century.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.520
Then, he worked through works

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.340
on a particular object or book,

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.670
and more recently multiple
projects on rituality,

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:18.690
visual culture, historiography,
and print culture.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.940
His books are exquisitely produced.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.120
For example, "The Chinese Voices
in the Rights Controversy"

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.290
has a whole series of
wonderful reproductions

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.060
of Chinese texts in color,

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.553
and some of them fold out in full form.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.540
Both in keeping with
his research interests

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.060
and also, I suspect, by being a Jesuit

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:40.983
and having access to nice presses.

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:46.353
We were discussing at
work, at lunchtime, today,

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.220
his work on "L'autre dans la mission,"

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.320
after (speaks French).

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:59.150
Again, how dance is a moral
national interest in China,

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:00.840
in early China,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.000
and how the pauses and the silences

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.470
are ritually important too.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.590
And again, you can see all
these wonderful illustrations

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.353
of dance figures throughout the work.

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:15.060
Professor Standaert has not
only given great service

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:18.450
to the field in his books,
but also in other aspects.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.690
He curates the EU China list, email list,

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:24.990
that many of us receive
details every few days

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:28.980
of new articles and new
books produced in this field.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.650
He's also been a curriculum innovator,

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:34.413
creating service courses
throughout Belgium.

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:38.250
So super delighted to have
you here speaking to us today,

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:39.870
and we're looking forward to your lecture

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.540
on "The Interconnected
History of the Chinese Gazette

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:45.063
in the 18th Century." Welcome.

00:04:45.063 --> 00:04:48.230
(audience applauding)

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.440
- Thank you very much for
this very kind introduction.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.030
I'm delighted to be here

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.313
and very honored also to be here.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:05.930
I also thank the foundation
to support this visit

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:10.260
and also the center to welcome me.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.700
I'm coming from a food
culture and a gift culture,

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:19.020
so I also brought chocolate Easter eggs.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:22.350
So during the break at the end,

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:25.080
you can take them and make sure

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.730
that at the end of the
reception, nothing is left over.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:31.713
I'm not going to carry them home.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:36.520
Let me immediately start with

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.767
the talk, looking at China
from the periphery...

00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:44.790
"The Interconnected
History of Chinese Gazette

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.460
in the 18th Century."

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:51.030
And I first start with
a bit a personal note.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:53.010
You already referred to it.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:57.300
So my main interest in research

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.167
is this very simple question,

00:05:59.167 --> 00:06:02.607
"What happens when two
cultures meet each other?"

00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:08.220
When I was taking my
Sinology classes in Leiden,

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:11.010
I had some courses in anthropology,

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:14.760
and that was all about
contact between cultures.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.040
That was a specialty also in Leiden.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.150
And I was fascinated by that topic.

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:25.350
And I also had the chance to have

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:28.233
Eric Zurcher as a professor.

00:06:29.103 --> 00:06:33.840
Eric Zurcher, who was also
the Hume lecturer in 2003.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:37.590
So that's more than 20 years ago.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:41.910
And Professor Zurcher
is known for his studies

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:44.520
on Buddhism in China,

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.640
but also on Christianity in China.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:51.780
Basically, he had two
books and 60 articles,

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:54.570
and half of them are on Buddhism

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:57.480
and half on Christianity.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:59.437
And his major question was,

00:06:59.437 --> 00:07:02.940
"What happens when
Buddhism arrived in China,

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:06.510
and how was it transformed
by Chinese society?"

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:09.847
Though the title does not
really sound like that,

00:07:09.847 --> 00:07:11.700
"The Buddhist Conquest of China."

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.860
In fact, he was approaching the question

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:15.900
from the other side.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.090
And having studied that subject,

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:21.990
he was wondering what happened
with another "minority"

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:26.310
religion, like Christianity
in China, in the 17th century.

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:28.770
So I attended his seminar,

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:32.760
and then he encouraged
me to pursue that topic.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:36.330
So that's a bit of the
origin of my research.

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:38.883
And in one of the interviews he gave,

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:41.643
people asked him, "But, why?

00:07:42.644 --> 00:07:44.580
Why do you study this question

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:46.200
and also Christianity in China?"

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:48.960
And he answered, "I believe
that Chinese culture

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:51.180
shows its futures most clearly

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:53.850
when it is confronted with
something from outside.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:55.920
It's like people in conflict.

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.720
When you are quarreling
with your neighbor,

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:00.270
you may see things and show things

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:01.890
about your character

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:03.870
that you otherwise never would.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.600
In the same way, the Chinese have shown

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:08.730
certain characteristic features

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:12.660
in their reactions to
Buddhism and Christianity."

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:16.567
So his major, yeah, concern was, yeah,

00:08:16.567 --> 00:08:21.210
"I want to understand
China as a sinologist."

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:25.050
And that's also my primary interest

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:28.710
through an inter-contact between cultures.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:31.320
And to give my, yeah...

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:35.430
To put that in a larger context,
because I put as a title,

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:38.640
looking at China from the periphery,

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:40.320
I think about 10 years ago,

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:45.320
we made a major invention
in the photography history

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:48.963
because we invented the selfie.

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:52.680
And by inventing the selfie,

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:57.510
we created the illusion
that we no longer need

00:08:57.510 --> 00:09:01.470
something that was
essential for photography.

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:03.760
Namely that it was always the other

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:07.290
who made a picture of myself.

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:10.320
And now we think that when we
take a picture of ourselves,

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:12.750
we only need ourselves.

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:16.200
And that's a kind of challenge,
I think, for history.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:21.200
How can that relate to
the sentence of Turk also?

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:24.090
How can maybe... It's not the only way,

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:29.090
but how can also how, like,
looks or how, like, sources,

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:31.863
tell something about China?

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:37.030
And this is related to
a concept of interaction

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:42.360
on which, yeah, I'm doing
further research at this moment.

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.270
I just do small,

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:48.690
let's say theoretical break,
on this in-betweenness.

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:50.137
So if you would ask me,

00:09:50.137 --> 00:09:55.137
"What is my vision on
intercultural history at..."

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:58.383
And I take the case of
the 17th, 18th century.

00:09:59.393 --> 00:10:00.942
To put it very general,

00:10:00.942 --> 00:10:03.360
you have the Chinese,
you have the Europeans,

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:04.530
and they interact.

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:08.545
And then there is a kind of
text, (speaks foreign language)

00:10:08.545 --> 00:10:09.877
the mathematics and we wonder,

00:10:09.877 --> 00:10:12.480
"Is this now Chinese? Is this European?"

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:16.440
Whereas what I am interested
in is that, in fact,

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:17.890
this is an

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:23.070
result of an interaction
and an in-betweenness,

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:27.003
and any in-betweenness
can lead to a story.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:30.690
Just like in this meeting, we meet.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:32.700
This is a kind of interaction.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:34.740
And after that, at the reception,

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:37.620
you can start to tell a
story or your question,

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:39.360
and you send an email to someone,

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.527
or I will tell something to others,

00:10:41.527 --> 00:10:42.750
"How was it?" And so on.

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:44.460
It's always a story.

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:49.460
And this is, or can be, expressed
in new cultural creations,

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:55.020
such as text, images,
artifacts, buildings,

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:58.530
but also in rituals,
communities, concepts.

00:10:58.530 --> 00:11:00.570
Now, as historians,

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:05.070
we have access to these
cultural creations.

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:06.390
And to a certain extent,

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:10.530
maybe our dream is also to get some sense

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:14.940
through the story of the
original in-betweenness

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:17.640
or the interaction that took place.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:21.510
But it's even more important
is that the same interaction

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:23.910
is also between our documents

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:27.990
and the way how we look at them.

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:32.130
So this is a little bit the
frame in which I try to reflect,

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:37.020
but now I will only focus
on two aspects of it.

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:41.490
Namely first, looking at the
culture from the periphery.

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:44.430
This is nothing original, I think.

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:47.340
For instance, at Fudan University,

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:48.420
you have this institute

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:52.380
for higher advanced humanistic studies.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:55.461
And that's one also of the teams.

00:11:55.461 --> 00:11:57.570
(speaks foreign language)

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:02.570
So on the basis of written
material, other views,

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:07.140
what is said about Chinese
history, for instance.

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:11.040
And the whole idea expressed
by several scholars,

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:14.400
also here at the university here,

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:17.193
about interconnected cultural history.

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:19.980
So what will be my case?

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:23.133
My case will be the Chinese gazette.

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:27.630
And as you can see, this is a drawing

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:30.000
of a photograph, not...

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.883
But a drawing by an Italian,

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:37.860
who made the drawing of
this grand counselor.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:41.220
And this is the only image
that is still preserved.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:43.257
And you can see here's the other

00:12:43.257 --> 00:12:47.040
who makes the picture of this person.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:51.120
So this latest book also to involve

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:53.377
a little bit my personal history.

00:12:53.377 --> 00:12:58.377
I have a project on print
in 17th and 18th century,

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.030
because when the missionaries
arrived in China,

00:13:03.030 --> 00:13:05.580
unlike in Latin America, in India,

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.080
in the Philippines, and so on,

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:12.660
there was an active
printing culture in China.

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:15.240
And not only Matteo Ricci says that it

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:18.337
is cheaper, faster, and more
efficient than in Europe,

00:13:18.337 --> 00:13:23.337
but there was no pre-printing censorship.

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:27.900
So they immediately could
publish what they wanted.

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:32.137
Within this context, I was
raising the simple question,

00:13:32.137 --> 00:13:37.137
"Did the missionaries
also read the gazette?"

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:39.090
I come back on the gazette,

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:44.090
the official newspaper
with official decisions.

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:48.450
And I had hoped to spend maybe two weeks

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:50.257
on this topic because I said,

00:13:50.257 --> 00:13:53.160
"Well, there will be not
so much material about it."

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:55.530
And I spent three years on it.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:14:00.530
So this was a kind of serendipity.

00:14:00.630 --> 00:14:01.890
And then Covid came,

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:04.260
so I had the occasion to work

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:07.020
a bit harder to produce a book.

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:11.240
And this Covid also affected one of...

00:14:12.201 --> 00:14:14.100
Moriga who is sitting there,

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:16.530
because she had the scholarship for Yale,

00:14:16.530 --> 00:14:19.050
and she could not leave for Yale.

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:20.790
And now she's here.

00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:22.650
But her state was delighted.

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:24.397
And when she told her that, I said,

00:14:24.397 --> 00:14:27.030
"Well, you come and work for
me and you will help me."

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:30.510
And so this book is also
partly with great help

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:35.400
of Moriga by criticizing my text.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:42.150
So we... I will focus on this gazette.

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:43.317
And what is a gazette?

00:14:43.317 --> 00:14:47.100
"It is a publicly available
government gazette

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:50.850
that was distributed and sold in print,

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:53.430
and sometimes also manuscripts."

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:58.430
And generally, it is called
"dibao" or "chaobao,"

00:14:58.676 --> 00:15:01.410
or sometimes also "jingbao."

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:03.450
Or let's say in Western literature,

00:15:03.450 --> 00:15:05.670
it's most often known as jingbao,

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:07.440
the Peking gazette.

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:08.910
Now, since the 90s,

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:13.440
there have been a number of
publications on this topic,

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:15.150
mainly also by Chinese scholars.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:17.010
You find here the references.

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:22.010
And Morcrose, in one of her
articles, she mentions...

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:23.820
Or in a dissertation, she mentions

00:15:23.820 --> 00:15:27.150
there are three ways in
which the gazette is studied:

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:28.590
history of the press,

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:30.240
or history of the state,

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:33.240
or history of the information in China.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:38.240
And here are three examples
of these approaches.

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:40.300
Barbara Mittler

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:45.490
treated this topic among her work

00:15:46.347 --> 00:15:48.570
in the context of a newspaper.

00:15:48.570 --> 00:15:50.763
And so then, you'll
link it to a newspaper.

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:55.410
Hilda De Weerdt on the
distribution of information

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:57.120
in the Soong Dynasty.

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:00.540
And Emily Mokros has recently this book

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:03.513
where the link with the state is made.

00:16:04.590 --> 00:16:08.433
And for the period after 1800,

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:14.130
the information is quite extensive.

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:16.170
Also in English,

00:16:16.170 --> 00:16:19.380
because quite a number of missionaries

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:24.030
translated these Chinese
jingbao into English.

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:27.990
And there is a whole
database on it by Brill.

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:32.843
And also the reader that
is published also by Brill.

00:16:33.810 --> 00:16:35.100
Now, what is the problem?

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:39.930
The problem is that hardly
any Chinese gazettes

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:43.443
are preserved from before 1800.

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:49.143
Basically, for instance,
for the Qianlong period,

00:16:50.993 --> 00:16:54.457
in that 60 years, you
only have 394 gazettes,

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:59.580
which means 394 days,

00:16:59.580 --> 00:17:04.050
which means, more or less,
one year and a half of text.

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:06.150
So to compare that with Europe,

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:10.410
where the newspapers, or
what we call newspapers,

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:12.570
existed since the end of the 16th...

00:17:12.570 --> 00:17:15.570
In fact, the early 17th century.

00:17:15.570 --> 00:17:18.060
Since the 1720s, yeah,

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:22.800
you have quite a extensive
documentation versions

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:26.943
of gazettes of the late
17th century and so on.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:31.500
Here you have also from
the Yongzheng period,

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:32.910
and that's called dibao,

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:35.910
but that is, in fact,
not an original gazette.

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:40.320
And it's the title that
is used later to put it

00:17:40.320 --> 00:17:41.730
and to make it as a reference,

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:45.360
but it's not an original text.

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:48.693
So what is then the next problem?

00:17:48.693 --> 00:17:53.693
It's that most scholars will use the name,

00:17:53.977 --> 00:17:56.340
"jingbao, Peking Gazette,"

00:17:56.340 --> 00:17:58.650
and also the structure is one

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:03.650
of the gazette after
1800 for what is before.

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:11.030
One solution is to check
for references in Chinese.

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:13.890
Diaries, notebooks, and novels.

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:16.170
As for instance, Hilda De Weerdt has done

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:18.153
with other people as well.

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:21.637
So my simple question was then,

00:18:21.637 --> 00:18:25.870
"Can we find some references
about the pre-1800

00:18:27.023 --> 00:18:32.023
gazette in this periphery,
namely in European,

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:37.460
or as I will refer to, in Korean sources?"

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:39.240
This was not completely new

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:40.950
because there are two other scholars

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:44.100
who briefly have done some research that,

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:47.190
especially NII Yoko for the

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:49.500
Amyu Period.

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:54.500
So what will be my basic
statement during this talk?

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:58.623
So regarding looking at the
culture from the periphery,

00:18:59.610 --> 00:19:01.950
I will try to convince you that, indeed,

00:19:01.950 --> 00:19:05.850
European sources expand our knowledge

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:09.120
on the gazette before 1800

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:12.840
and disregarding to the structure
of the content and so on.

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:16.050
And moreover, that they inform us about

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:20.790
how Europeans read the gazette,

00:19:20.790 --> 00:19:25.503
and also as global agents
informed Europe about the gazette.

00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:28.320
And then the next step will be

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:30.960
a kind of interconnected history,

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:34.440
namely that if we looked and used

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:36.180
all kind of digital databases,

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:40.590
we can discover how
these translated versions

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:44.790
of the gazette also were
received, for instance,

00:19:44.790 --> 00:19:49.230
by enlightenment thinkers and developed...

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:52.980
Helped to develop the thought
about not only about China,

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:56.670
but about European politics.

00:19:56.670 --> 00:19:59.463
So that will be my major statement.

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:03.333
Now, I had to solve a first problem.

00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:07.650
Namely when I saw translations

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:10.440
or quotations from the gazette

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:13.840
was to verify whether

00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:19.350
these were correct statements.

00:20:19.350 --> 00:20:22.560
Was this invented by the missionaries,

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:24.930
or was this really...

00:20:24.930 --> 00:20:27.570
Can that be found in a gazette?

00:20:27.570 --> 00:20:29.190
So you have the gazette,

00:20:29.190 --> 00:20:31.740
and then you have the Western manuscript,

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:33.777
and then you have the printed text.

00:20:33.777 --> 00:20:37.260
But the problem is that these gazettes,

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:40.980
except for eight copies,

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:42.693
are no longer preserved.

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:45.423
So what did I do?

00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:48.600
I took a sideway,

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:52.800
and I first tried to find the

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:55.493
references in the Shalu.

00:20:57.510 --> 00:21:01.890
But the Shalu is not the
origin of the gazette

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:04.410
because the Shalu was often compared

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:08.130
later than the gazette was.

00:21:08.130 --> 00:21:10.380
Let's say it was printed within...

00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:13.470
Or distributed within a month after

00:21:13.470 --> 00:21:15.423
the event took place in the palace.

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:19.920
So, but on the basis of the Shalu,

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:23.430
I could identify the same passages

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:26.490
in the (speaks mandarin) or
in the (speaks mandarin).

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:27.810
the edicts by the emperor.

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:32.810
And they were a much
closer, reliable source

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:36.630
to what was taken over in the gazette.

00:21:36.630 --> 00:21:38.790
Because what happened at court,

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:41.970
the decisions were then written
and then put on a board.

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:45.570
And then people copied them,
and then they were printed.

00:21:45.570 --> 00:21:48.720
So to make this concrete,
this is a manuscript.

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:51.420
And there you will see...

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:53.790
I can't see what we here.

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:58.790
The text here and there. It's
written (speaks Mandarin)

00:21:59.610 --> 00:22:03.150
Whatever, (speaks Mandarin)

00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:04.650
So I see this figure,

00:22:04.650 --> 00:22:08.460
and then I try to find in the academia,

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:13.200
the Scripta Sinica database in the Shalu,

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:15.360
if I can identify that.

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:18.810
And indeed, here you
have this figure here,

00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:23.040
and I can identify this name
in the French transcription

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:26.457
of the 17th or the 18th century and so on.

00:22:26.457 --> 00:22:29.190
And then on the basis of this,

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:31.950
this is the (speaks Mandarin)

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:35.670
and these are the original edicts.

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:39.180
And the same here with the passage

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:42.330
about Russian and the famous question.

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:46.680
And I could identify the sources.

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:51.680
So just like the collection
in Brill for the post-1800,

00:22:52.017 --> 00:22:57.017
and the reader, so Brill asked
me to include it in my book.

00:22:57.060 --> 00:23:00.300
I have about 60 pages identifying

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:04.440
more than 300 items of...

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:07.320
That were translated
in one or another text

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:08.940
in European text.

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:12.270
And that can be linked to original events

00:23:12.270 --> 00:23:14.193
that you always can check.

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:19.440
So what I shall do now, in my book,

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:23.550
I discuss the country
period, the Yongzheng Period,

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:25.260
and then the Qianlong Period.

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:27.330
I will focus on the Yongzheng period,

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:31.593
which is most interesting
in terms of the interaction.

00:23:32.790 --> 00:23:35.613
And let us start with the production,

00:23:36.810 --> 00:23:38.610
the printed sources.

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:41.940
What are the most
important printed sources?

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:46.940
The first appears in the "Lettres
Edifiantes Et Curieuses,"

00:23:47.310 --> 00:23:49.597
which is a major collection in Europe

00:23:49.597 --> 00:23:54.240
in the 18th century with 34 volumes.

00:23:54.240 --> 00:23:58.770
And in it, you can find
three letters in total,

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:03.770
more than 300 pages by a certain
missionary, Cyr Contancin.

00:24:05.160 --> 00:24:06.360
He's not known at all.

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:08.940
He has no text in Chinese.

00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:11.940
He hardly had any other Lettres.

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:14.100
But he has these three Lettres

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:17.223
that are completely
devoted to the gazette.

00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:20.823
And he arrived in China in 1701,

00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:23.730
spent seven years in Beijing,

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:26.190
and then he was an exile in Canton,

00:24:26.190 --> 00:24:28.260
and that's where he did the work.

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:29.580
He came back to Europe.

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:33.390
He worked with Du Halde on
"Description... de la Chine."

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:36.140
And then he left back to
China, but he died on the way.

00:24:37.476 --> 00:24:41.500
Now in this text, he gives

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:46.290
information about the
content of the gazette.

00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:49.530
And he says, "Well, you have
the names of the officials,

00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:54.000
criminal cases, admonitions,
rituals, laws, and so on."

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:55.890
When you look at this list,

00:24:55.890 --> 00:24:59.550
and you compare that with
descriptions today in Chinese

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:02.550
or in Western sources about the gazette,

00:25:02.550 --> 00:25:04.080
it's very, very similar.

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:06.090
And so yeah, the good understanding

00:25:06.090 --> 00:25:08.700
of what was the gazette.

00:25:08.700 --> 00:25:10.830
And I did the same work.

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:13.233
I come back on this text later,

00:25:14.130 --> 00:25:16.740
which of course is Lettres.

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:19.920
And you can see here, again, the case.

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:21.630
This is a (speaks Mandarin)

00:25:21.630 --> 00:25:24.390
but that's the French translation...

00:25:24.390 --> 00:25:29.283
Transcription in the 17th century.

00:25:31.350 --> 00:25:35.200
To give an example of
what kind of events is

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:38.640
translated and reported,

00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:40.597
here you have an overview.

00:25:40.597 --> 00:25:43.710
"The mourning and installation
of the new empress,

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:46.620
a memorial archway,

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:50.310
the rescue of people in
years of bad harvest,

00:25:50.310 --> 00:25:53.940
the relief of the poor,
death sentence," and so on.

00:25:53.940 --> 00:25:54.930
So as you can see,

00:25:54.930 --> 00:25:57.693
these topics are not
related to Christianity.

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:00.720
And in the 300 pages,

00:26:00.720 --> 00:26:03.420
he hardly has any references

00:26:03.420 --> 00:26:06.663
to Christianity in an explicit way.

00:26:10.980 --> 00:26:13.380
Next, he gives information
about the structure

00:26:13.380 --> 00:26:14.960
and the form of the...

00:26:16.410 --> 00:26:18.120
Of the gazette.

00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:20.850
And here you can see, the memorials,

00:26:20.850 --> 00:26:22.860
the answers by the emperor,

00:26:22.860 --> 00:26:24.360
the instructions of the emperor,

00:26:24.360 --> 00:26:26.160
other memorials as well.

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:29.610
And he also adds that they contain more

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:33.300
60 to 70 pages.

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:34.800
That's a little bit of a problem,

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:38.763
but that can be solved
in one way or another.

00:26:41.130 --> 00:26:45.760
Do we have a gazette of
the Yongzheng period?

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:51.800
Well this is, in my eyes,
the only copy that is left,

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:55.170
or that I could identify.

00:26:55.170 --> 00:26:57.507
And this can be found in the

00:26:57.507 --> 00:27:01.353
Osterreichische
Nationalbibliothek in Vienna.

00:27:02.730 --> 00:27:07.730
And as you can see, the
title is "Tizou Quanlu."

00:27:09.710 --> 00:27:10.593
You find it here.

00:27:11.460 --> 00:27:14.700
And you have the structure of the gazette,

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:18.240
and then first decisions
here and then it continues.

00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:20.250
You have the different departments.

00:27:20.250 --> 00:27:23.010
So this has the whole structure.

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:24.630
Now...

00:27:24.630 --> 00:27:29.630
Why is this probably the only copy left?

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:35.090
And moreover, is this the name
of the gazette at the time?

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:41.010
I checked in plenty of
databases on "Tizou Quanlu,"

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:44.883
and I could not find any
reference in a Chinese database.

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:49.620
Only in Korean databases.

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:54.420
In Korean databases of
this (speaks Mandarin)

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:57.600
These are the reports of the delegations

00:27:57.600 --> 00:28:01.110
that annually from Korea went to China.

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:05.970
And there you have reports and
quotations from the gazette.

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:08.340
And they said, "Oh, today
we read this and this

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:10.580
in the 'Tizou Quanlu.'"

00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:14.557
Moreover, they add in one of the sections,

00:28:14.557 --> 00:28:18.840
"And this is the same as our chaobao"

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:21.873
So they really make this link.

00:28:22.740 --> 00:28:24.630
Maybe we have a second copy left,

00:28:24.630 --> 00:28:26.403
and that's in this box here.

00:28:27.390 --> 00:28:29.150
So that's in the museum in...

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:33.750
Museum national d'histoire
naturelle in Paris.

00:28:33.750 --> 00:28:36.820
And they have a collection
of stones and of

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:40.173
mineral collection.

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:44.940
And most probably these
minerals are wrapped

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:47.640
in paper from the gazette.

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:50.400
I already asked them,
"Yeah, could we open it?"

00:28:50.400 --> 00:28:53.460
But they still have to discuss that.

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:55.980
But I have to convince them,

00:28:55.980 --> 00:28:57.690
because they may have the second copy

00:28:57.690 --> 00:29:01.100
of gazette in the world. So...

00:29:02.910 --> 00:29:06.030
Now, so you wonder, "Yeah,
but this is not Chengbao...

00:29:06.030 --> 00:29:07.707
Or this is not (speaks Mandarin)"

00:29:09.047 --> 00:29:12.833
So there was already a very
important article in '92

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:17.397
in Wen Wu by Pan Tien Shen,

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:24.060
saying that, "Yeah, we are always using

00:29:24.060 --> 00:29:25.620
this term (speaks Mandarin),

00:29:25.620 --> 00:29:29.260
but (speaks Mandarin)
is only used since 1852

00:29:30.930 --> 00:29:33.357
with a few exceptions before."

00:29:34.230 --> 00:29:39.230
And the term used in the Qianlong period

00:29:39.270 --> 00:29:40.520
is the (speaks Mandarin).

00:29:43.585 --> 00:29:44.910
That's this here.

00:29:44.910 --> 00:29:48.810
And here you have some copies
of the (speaks Mandarin)

00:29:48.810 --> 00:29:50.370
that are preserved in Paris,

00:29:50.370 --> 00:29:54.960
and of which know that Amyu...

00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:55.800
He used it in here.

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:59.943
You have the handwriting
of Amyu in the top.

00:30:01.470 --> 00:30:04.320
So what is the conclusion then?

00:30:04.320 --> 00:30:07.950
Provisional conclusion of
this research is that, yeah,

00:30:07.950 --> 00:30:12.480
the identification of this
text, of the "Tizou Quanlu,"

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:15.597
that this is kind of forerunner
of the (speaks Mandarin),

00:30:16.800 --> 00:30:19.100
which later then became
the (speaks Mandarin).

00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:24.480
And this is one of the examples how

00:30:24.480 --> 00:30:26.670
by looking from the periphery,

00:30:26.670 --> 00:30:31.670
you can discover something
about Chinese sources.

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:35.160
You also know this text because here

00:30:35.160 --> 00:30:37.650
in Yale there is this project,

00:30:37.650 --> 00:30:41.250
in which I don't know whether you look...

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:43.290
I don't know.

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:45.540
See we... But anyway.

00:30:45.540 --> 00:30:48.313
So you have the references to it.

00:30:48.313 --> 00:30:51.977
And it was also thanks to Martin Heijdra

00:30:51.977 --> 00:30:53.850
with whom I was in contact.

00:30:53.850 --> 00:30:58.200
That he made me attentive
to the (speaks Mandarin)

00:30:58.200 --> 00:31:01.173
and so that I also
discovered them in Paris.

00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:04.890
So we go back to Contancin.

00:31:04.890 --> 00:31:06.840
He also tells something about

00:31:06.840 --> 00:31:09.003
the significance of the gazette.

00:31:10.140 --> 00:31:14.210
So, and this is a team that
will come back and back.

00:31:14.210 --> 00:31:17.670
He said, "They are very useful.

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:19.170
Instead of filling it,

00:31:19.170 --> 00:31:21.390
as is done in certain parts of Europe,

00:31:21.390 --> 00:31:25.950
with useless things and
slander and calumny.

00:31:25.950 --> 00:31:27.750
In China, it only mentioned

00:31:27.750 --> 00:31:30.057
what relates to the emperor."

00:31:31.500 --> 00:31:34.500
In Europe, we have newspapers with gossip.

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:38.040
But in China, this is reliable material.

00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:40.770
And that's why it can
also be an instruction

00:31:40.770 --> 00:31:43.620
for literati and the People.

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:46.170
And then he adds the following.

00:31:46.170 --> 00:31:49.650
I will read the whole sentence
because it's important

00:31:49.650 --> 00:31:51.007
for what we'll follow.

00:31:51.007 --> 00:31:54.030
"Nothing is printed here
which has not been presented

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:57.360
to the Emperor, or which does not come

00:31:57.360 --> 00:31:59.250
from the Emperor himself.

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:00.750
Those who take care of it,

00:32:00.750 --> 00:32:03.420
would not dare to add anything to it,

00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:06.840
not even their own reflections,

00:32:06.840 --> 00:32:09.303
under the penalty of corporal punishment.

00:32:10.380 --> 00:32:13.770
Last year, two writers
were sentenced to death

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:16.170
for having inserted in the Gazette

00:32:16.170 --> 00:32:19.560
some circumstances which were false."

00:32:19.560 --> 00:32:24.560
So this is only one
sentence in this 300 pages,

00:32:24.720 --> 00:32:27.623
and I spent a lot of time to know

00:32:27.623 --> 00:32:29.460
whether that was correct or not.

00:32:29.460 --> 00:32:32.310
Finally, I found the reference,

00:32:32.310 --> 00:32:34.470
and indeed there was a case,

00:32:34.470 --> 00:32:37.710
that is mentioned also in the Shalu,

00:32:37.710 --> 00:32:41.940
where the Emperor had a meeting
with some of his counselors.

00:32:41.940 --> 00:32:45.720
And afterwards, they went out on a boat

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:47.850
in the summer palace,

00:32:47.850 --> 00:32:49.140
and they got drink,

00:32:49.140 --> 00:32:52.890
and they were drinking wine and whatever,

00:32:52.890 --> 00:32:54.900
and they had a good feast.

00:32:54.900 --> 00:32:58.980
And two of them, two of these reporters,

00:32:58.980 --> 00:33:02.510
in the xiaobao probably, mention...

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:05.160
Mention what happened.

00:33:05.160 --> 00:33:08.850
And then the Emperor
said, "No, it's not true

00:33:08.850 --> 00:33:10.820
because I never drink wine." And...

00:33:11.970 --> 00:33:13.443
They were condemned to death.

00:33:14.700 --> 00:33:17.643
So we know that this was a real case also.

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:21.630
There are also some
personal considerations

00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:23.460
throughout the concern in the gazette.

00:33:23.460 --> 00:33:25.293
It's useful to a missionary,

00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:29.013
and he regrets that for years.

00:33:29.977 --> 00:33:31.920
"I did not treat the newspaper.

00:33:31.920 --> 00:33:33.900
And now when I'm in the South,

00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:35.700
I see how interesting it is

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:38.937
for religion and laws and
customs and language."

00:33:40.020 --> 00:33:43.200
And most of the missionaries don't do so

00:33:43.200 --> 00:33:45.090
because the language is not enough.

00:33:45.090 --> 00:33:47.670
Or they immediately, the idea of...

00:33:47.670 --> 00:33:52.080
Associate the idea of gazette
with a European gazette.

00:33:52.080 --> 00:33:54.380
They imagine that it...

00:33:55.260 --> 00:33:58.380
In which one puts or has
put everything one wants,

00:33:58.380 --> 00:34:00.660
good or bad, without any distinction.

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:03.810
And you see their view
on European gazettes

00:34:03.810 --> 00:34:05.523
in contrast with China.

00:34:06.390 --> 00:34:10.624
So this is a first source, 300 pages.

00:34:10.624 --> 00:34:12.000
(speaks French)

00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:15.450
Second source is Perroni,

00:34:15.450 --> 00:34:18.429
and that is a person for...

00:34:18.429 --> 00:34:21.300
Who was procreator of "Propaganda Fide."

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:24.660
And he was also, at the
same time, in Canton.

00:34:24.660 --> 00:34:27.480
So probably they also even work together.

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:30.510
And he sends a yearly report to Rome

00:34:30.510 --> 00:34:32.040
for the "Propaganda Fide,"

00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:36.867
and one finds them in the
archives of the "Propaganda Fide."

00:34:38.190 --> 00:34:42.850
What is interesting here is
that we have a transcript

00:34:43.860 --> 00:34:45.960
of the whole text in Chinese.

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:48.480
That's really unique, on which we can...

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:50.190
We don't have the original gazette,

00:34:50.190 --> 00:34:51.663
but we have the transcript.

00:34:53.100 --> 00:34:57.153
The content, the form, is
very similar to Constancin.

00:34:58.170 --> 00:35:00.490
The difference, you can say, is that

00:35:01.590 --> 00:35:04.550
Constancin addresses it to Du Halde

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:06.720
to be widely spread.

00:35:06.720 --> 00:35:09.600
Perroni is only for the "Propaganda Fide."

00:35:09.600 --> 00:35:12.240
It was not published at all.

00:35:12.240 --> 00:35:16.020
What is unique is the
presence of this Chinese text.

00:35:16.020 --> 00:35:18.690
He also has some personal considerations,

00:35:18.690 --> 00:35:20.370
and what do you find?

00:35:20.370 --> 00:35:23.760
Yeah, the same references
nor is there any danger

00:35:23.760 --> 00:35:27.690
that the story will be altered
by a passion of the writer.

00:35:27.690 --> 00:35:31.620
And that happened... That someone was...

00:35:31.620 --> 00:35:34.233
Two people were condemned for it.

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:36.143
Now...

00:35:38.190 --> 00:35:41.610
Both of them, they mainly translate,

00:35:41.610 --> 00:35:46.140
but in between, one will
find some references

00:35:46.140 --> 00:35:48.540
with their idea on the emperor

00:35:48.540 --> 00:35:51.303
and about the bureaucratic system.

00:35:52.290 --> 00:35:55.530
So this is not a major topic.

00:35:55.530 --> 00:35:59.220
Maybe for the first letter
it is, but not in the other.

00:35:59.220 --> 00:36:03.240
But it transpires, and
between the translations,

00:36:03.240 --> 00:36:06.750
one will find some few
sentences discussing that.

00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:11.370
Now, what is the general idea of Contancin

00:36:11.370 --> 00:36:14.160
regarding the emperor?

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:18.210
So despite the fact that
Christianity is persecuted

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:21.360
and that he, himself, is in exile,

00:36:21.360 --> 00:36:26.360
it's a rather laudatory
description of the emperor.

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:27.990
He has a wise government,

00:36:27.990 --> 00:36:30.330
service to the public utility.

00:36:30.330 --> 00:36:32.430
He uses really this public utility,

00:36:32.430 --> 00:36:35.160
which was a team also in France.

00:36:35.160 --> 00:36:39.090
An active bureaucracy and...

00:36:39.090 --> 00:36:41.700
With officials taking initiative.

00:36:41.700 --> 00:36:45.930
Important to underscore that
not all the other Jesuits

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:50.160
had the same opinion,
because Gaubil and Du Halde,

00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:52.560
they were much more critical.

00:36:52.560 --> 00:36:56.220
They considerate it despotic government

00:36:56.220 --> 00:36:58.710
and an absolute and feared master.

00:36:58.710 --> 00:37:01.080
I come back on that topic later.

00:37:01.080 --> 00:37:05.340
Perroni has a (speaks foreign language)

00:37:05.340 --> 00:37:06.510
because on the one hand,

00:37:06.510 --> 00:37:10.620
he is positive the emperor
is good at lecturing,

00:37:10.620 --> 00:37:13.290
he is attentive to the
needs of the people.

00:37:13.290 --> 00:37:16.260
There is a bureaucracy
that has good communication

00:37:16.260 --> 00:37:17.880
with him and so on.

00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:21.360
On the other hand, he expresses the idea

00:37:21.360 --> 00:37:24.420
that the emperor is
blinded by his officials.

00:37:24.420 --> 00:37:27.420
His speeches are
disconnected from reality.

00:37:27.420 --> 00:37:30.810
The bureaucracy is perpetually flattering

00:37:30.810 --> 00:37:32.673
and praising the emperor.

00:37:34.380 --> 00:37:39.380
So what do we learn from
this production, you can say?

00:37:39.990 --> 00:37:44.700
Well that we already learned
something about the gazette,

00:37:44.700 --> 00:37:48.510
the existence of the gazette,
the form of the gazette,

00:37:48.510 --> 00:37:53.460
the extent of it, but also
something about the content

00:37:53.460 --> 00:37:56.703
that is represented in this text.

00:37:58.140 --> 00:38:00.540
So that's the first
point I wanted to make.

00:38:00.540 --> 00:38:02.910
This is the presentation.

00:38:02.910 --> 00:38:07.620
Let us now look at how were
these text, this information,

00:38:07.620 --> 00:38:09.010
how was that distributed

00:38:10.320 --> 00:38:12.300
before looking at the reception

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:14.700
by the enlightenment thinkers?

00:38:14.700 --> 00:38:19.170
Distribution in the first
place by academic journals.

00:38:19.170 --> 00:38:20.683
So at that time in France, for instance,

00:38:20.683 --> 00:38:24.330
there were quite a number
of academic journals.

00:38:24.330 --> 00:38:27.000
And what is typical for
these academic journals,

00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:31.050
let's say academic or
intellectual or the republic,

00:38:31.050 --> 00:38:35.730
the letter, is that
they have book reviews.

00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:39.090
But the book reviews are
not, in the first place,

00:38:39.090 --> 00:38:41.160
a whole evaluation of a book,

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:46.160
but rather consists of very
large excerpts of the text.

00:38:47.760 --> 00:38:52.760
And that's important because
they discussed the text by...

00:38:53.250 --> 00:38:57.090
Or let's say they present
the text by Contancin.

00:38:57.090 --> 00:38:59.640
And for people who did not have access

00:38:59.640 --> 00:39:01.620
to "Lettres Edifiantes Et Curieuses."

00:39:01.620 --> 00:39:04.470
In fact, the information of the...

00:39:04.470 --> 00:39:07.020
About the gazette three times.

00:39:07.020 --> 00:39:09.030
Because there were three different volumes

00:39:09.030 --> 00:39:13.020
over a period of nearly seven, eight years

00:39:13.020 --> 00:39:15.817
were produced in, for instance,

00:39:15.817 --> 00:39:19.920
"Journal des scavans" of
"Memoires de Trevoux."

00:39:19.920 --> 00:39:23.250
And one find in them the
same vision, the content,

00:39:23.250 --> 00:39:25.510
the difference with European gazettes

00:39:25.510 --> 00:39:27.633
and the vision on the emperor.

00:39:28.500 --> 00:39:31.140
You also have the
newspapers, or the pamphlets.

00:39:31.140 --> 00:39:34.710
For instance here, "The Bee Revived,"

00:39:34.710 --> 00:39:38.520
which is a kind of pamphlet
that was only published

00:39:38.520 --> 00:39:40.650
during two or three years,

00:39:40.650 --> 00:39:44.880
based on the text that we
will see in a few minutes.

00:39:44.880 --> 00:39:47.490
And in which there is a whole description.

00:39:47.490 --> 00:39:49.380
The public gazette

00:39:49.380 --> 00:39:53.040
is the reverse of our European gazettes,

00:39:53.040 --> 00:39:55.890
which are medley of truth and falsehood.

00:39:55.890 --> 00:39:58.950
You see that these points are repeated.

00:39:58.950 --> 00:40:03.030
And the reference, again,
to these two writers

00:40:03.030 --> 00:40:04.653
that were content.

00:40:06.870 --> 00:40:11.767
So these are newspapers,
journals, also some books.

00:40:11.767 --> 00:40:15.180
"Description... de la Chine"
is an important production.

00:40:15.180 --> 00:40:17.970
Four volumes edited by Du Halde,

00:40:17.970 --> 00:40:21.210
and was edited twice in French,

00:40:21.210 --> 00:40:24.030
in France and in the Netherlands,

00:40:24.030 --> 00:40:27.840
in English, in German,
later also in Russian.

00:40:27.840 --> 00:40:32.767
And in it, one finds, again,
a description of the gazette.

00:40:32.767 --> 00:40:35.190
"There is nothing inserted in the gazette,

00:40:35.190 --> 00:40:38.910
but what has reference to the government."

00:40:38.910 --> 00:40:43.263
It's to direct and to instruct
the Mandarins and the people.

00:40:44.190 --> 00:40:48.750
And again, the reference in 1725,

00:40:48.750 --> 00:40:51.570
you had these authors who were...

00:40:51.570 --> 00:40:54.690
Writers who were condemned to death

00:40:54.690 --> 00:40:58.803
because they spread false information.

00:41:00.900 --> 00:41:03.500
We can look even further. You have...

00:41:04.680 --> 00:41:07.680
Works on Chinese and world history.

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:11.520
One of them is De Mailla,
"Histoire generale de la Chine."

00:41:11.520 --> 00:41:13.590
It's a remarkable work.

00:41:13.590 --> 00:41:16.683
More than 7,000 pages, 13 volumes.

00:41:17.580 --> 00:41:22.580
This is, until today, still
the most extensive work

00:41:23.160 --> 00:41:26.490
in any European language based on Chinese

00:41:26.490 --> 00:41:29.973
and Manchu sources written by one author.

00:41:31.200 --> 00:41:33.990
We have, of course, "The
Cambridge History of China."

00:41:33.990 --> 00:41:35.583
150 authors.

00:41:36.607 --> 00:41:40.920
I'm not sure that it
already has 13 volumes.

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:44.040
But these are nearly translations,

00:41:44.040 --> 00:41:47.040
or basically translations, of
the (speaks foreign language),

00:41:50.987 --> 00:41:54.600
in the Manchu version basically.

00:41:54.600 --> 00:41:57.140
And in it, it is...

00:41:58.590 --> 00:42:01.320
Includes also the Yongzheng period

00:42:01.320 --> 00:42:03.180
of which was not yet written

00:42:03.180 --> 00:42:06.090
the history in the
classical Chinese history.

00:42:06.090 --> 00:42:08.340
So they took all the information

00:42:08.340 --> 00:42:10.320
from the letters of Contancin

00:42:10.320 --> 00:42:12.840
to write that part of history.

00:42:12.840 --> 00:42:15.157
And other works, like de Marsy,

00:42:15.157 --> 00:42:17.490
"Histoire moderne des
Chinois, des Japonnois,

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:18.930
des Persan," etc.

00:42:18.930 --> 00:42:21.570
in which you also have a whole chapter,

00:42:21.570 --> 00:42:24.630
or whole part, on the gazettes.

00:42:24.630 --> 00:42:27.330
So this is the distribution.

00:42:27.330 --> 00:42:29.223
What could we say about it?

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:34.867
First, I drawn the idea
of Jurgen Osterhammel,

00:42:34.867 --> 00:42:38.073
this famous book in favoring the East.

00:42:42.060 --> 00:42:44.310
We can state, or I can state,

00:42:44.310 --> 00:42:46.860
that Europe was well
informed about the gazettes

00:42:46.860 --> 00:42:48.753
and about events in China.

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:52.710
And Osterhammel writes about this aspect

00:42:52.710 --> 00:42:54.450
around mid 18th century.

00:42:54.450 --> 00:42:56.190
The public in France, in Germany,

00:42:56.190 --> 00:42:57.990
was better informed about China

00:42:57.990 --> 00:43:02.990
than about many other countries
in the Europe's periphery.

00:43:03.030 --> 00:43:08.030
And I find that a quite
important statement.

00:43:08.250 --> 00:43:11.153
But yeah, you need
someone like Osterhammel,

00:43:11.153 --> 00:43:13.950
who has more or less read everything,

00:43:13.950 --> 00:43:16.410
don't make such a statement.

00:43:16.410 --> 00:43:18.930
But what is even more important

00:43:18.930 --> 00:43:21.720
is that Osterhammel
makes a clear, let's say,

00:43:21.720 --> 00:43:26.340
distinction between the
pre-1800 and post-1800

00:43:26.340 --> 00:43:30.420
way in which Europe speaks about the East.

00:43:30.420 --> 00:43:34.884
So he speaks about an
inclusive Eurocentrism...

00:43:34.884 --> 00:43:37.200
An exclusive Eurocentrism.

00:43:37.200 --> 00:43:38.370
And in the first period,

00:43:38.370 --> 00:43:41.940
what you have is that
quite a number of authors

00:43:41.940 --> 00:43:46.470
make an attempt to let the East,

00:43:46.470 --> 00:43:49.860
in this case, China, speak on the basis

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:54.330
of their own voices or texts.

00:43:54.330 --> 00:43:58.533
And you see that already in
Du Halde and in other texts.

00:43:59.460 --> 00:44:03.720
This... They really make it explicit.

00:44:03.720 --> 00:44:06.900
They say, "We present translations."

00:44:06.900 --> 00:44:09.840
Of course, translations are
always an interpretation.

00:44:09.840 --> 00:44:11.613
But "We present the translation

00:44:11.613 --> 00:44:14.997
because that's the best way
how you can understand China."

00:44:15.900 --> 00:44:19.260
And it's very few interpretations.

00:44:19.260 --> 00:44:22.410
So modern scholarship will focus morely

00:44:22.410 --> 00:44:26.310
on the misinterpretation
of China by the scholars,

00:44:26.310 --> 00:44:29.550
whereas maybe we have
to rediscover everything

00:44:29.550 --> 00:44:32.943
that was already translated at that time.

00:44:34.890 --> 00:44:38.010
So this was the distribution.

00:44:38.010 --> 00:44:41.430
So we notice here that,
in fact, the information

00:44:41.430 --> 00:44:45.360
that was, after all, relatively limited.

00:44:45.360 --> 00:44:47.460
The three letters of Contancin

00:44:47.460 --> 00:44:52.460
had a much wider distribution
in all kind of ways in Europe

00:44:52.980 --> 00:44:57.600
so that Europe was
informed about gazettes.

00:44:57.600 --> 00:45:02.100
But now what happened in
the interconnected history

00:45:02.100 --> 00:45:05.100
and how did enlightenment thinkers...

00:45:05.100 --> 00:45:09.033
Well, react or use or discuss the gazette.

00:45:13.290 --> 00:45:16.863
And we start with Voltaire.

00:45:17.700 --> 00:45:22.700
And here you see a quote
from a text that is entitled,

00:45:23.047 --> 00:45:26.910
"Gazette" in a dictionary.

00:45:26.910 --> 00:45:30.289
And that's in the famous
"Encylopedie ov Dictionnaire

00:45:30.289 --> 00:45:32.893
(speaks French)."

00:45:32.893 --> 00:45:35.647
"Encyclopedie ov Dictionnaire
Raisonne Des Sciences.

00:45:37.303 --> 00:45:38.850
Des Art Et Des Metiers,"

00:45:38.850 --> 00:45:43.477
and that is the famous
enlightenment dictionary,

00:45:43.477 --> 00:45:45.210
"Encyclopedie."

00:45:45.210 --> 00:45:50.210
In which anyone working on
enlightenment uses that.

00:45:50.520 --> 00:45:53.670
One of the basic text in which

00:45:53.670 --> 00:45:55.980
all the ideas were integrated.

00:45:55.980 --> 00:46:00.980
And Voltaire writes the text on gazette,

00:46:01.620 --> 00:46:05.340
in which he explains that the gazette

00:46:05.340 --> 00:46:10.340
was invented in Venice at the
end of the 16th century...

00:46:10.430 --> 00:46:12.360
Or the beginning of the 17th century,

00:46:12.360 --> 00:46:15.993
and that it is called
gazette because of gazzetta,

00:46:17.460 --> 00:46:20.970
which was a coin in Venice.

00:46:20.970 --> 00:46:23.040
And you had to pay that coin to have

00:46:23.040 --> 00:46:27.480
your one or two pages or
whatever of your newspaper.

00:46:27.480 --> 00:46:30.840
And then, he adds, "Such
journals have been established

00:46:30.840 --> 00:46:33.060
in China from time immemorial,

00:46:33.060 --> 00:46:35.400
and the gazette of the
empire is printed there

00:46:35.400 --> 00:46:37.707
every day by order of the court."

00:46:39.270 --> 00:46:44.270
And you see immediately it
makes the link to China,

00:46:45.360 --> 00:46:50.010
and it's not very clear what
is meant by time immemorial.

00:46:50.010 --> 00:46:53.910
But at least it's a long time, that it...

00:46:53.910 --> 00:46:58.230
Much longer than it happened in Europe.

00:46:58.230 --> 00:47:02.100
He had some other commentaries.
I'll come back on that.

00:47:02.100 --> 00:47:04.890
But the interpretation is quite difficult

00:47:04.890 --> 00:47:06.453
of these other sentences.

00:47:07.380 --> 00:47:09.260
Now, the next question is then...

00:47:10.440 --> 00:47:13.200
Voltaire makes reference to it,

00:47:13.200 --> 00:47:18.200
but did he actually read
the letters by Contancin?

00:47:19.770 --> 00:47:23.010
And there we have a very nice proof,

00:47:23.010 --> 00:47:27.420
because after Voltaire's death,

00:47:27.420 --> 00:47:32.407
this whole library moved to
St. Petersburg by Catherine.

00:47:34.350 --> 00:47:37.860
And in the 1960-70s,

00:47:37.860 --> 00:47:42.860
when it was not easy to
do research in Russia,

00:47:43.530 --> 00:47:46.140
at least interpretive research,

00:47:46.140 --> 00:47:49.410
you had Russian researchers
going through all the volumes

00:47:49.410 --> 00:47:54.410
and printing down all
the annotations by...

00:47:56.340 --> 00:48:00.030
By Voltaire through the volumes.

00:48:00.030 --> 00:48:03.570
And so I could check, and indeed,

00:48:03.570 --> 00:48:06.150
he has several annotations in the letters

00:48:06.150 --> 00:48:08.520
concerning the gazettes.

00:48:08.520 --> 00:48:11.760
And then you find them also in my book.

00:48:11.760 --> 00:48:15.300
You have the paper slips, the references.

00:48:15.300 --> 00:48:18.330
And this is what I mentioned earlier.

00:48:18.330 --> 00:48:23.010
And so here, Voltaire write...

00:48:23.010 --> 00:48:27.183
Or this is the case of this Mr. Chiyeou,

00:48:30.030 --> 00:48:32.930
which he discusses in one of his books.

00:48:32.930 --> 00:48:37.260
So we know that he
actually read the gazettes.

00:48:37.260 --> 00:48:41.797
He refers to it, in the gazettes,
in his most famous book,

00:48:41.797 --> 00:48:44.847
"Essai sur les moeurs et
l'esprit des nations,"

00:48:45.690 --> 00:48:49.140
which is a very, very important
book in terms of history,

00:48:49.140 --> 00:48:54.140
because it's the text that
puts China first in history.

00:48:55.080 --> 00:48:57.450
And it was a major revolution,

00:48:57.450 --> 00:48:59.490
because it's not a biblical text,

00:48:59.490 --> 00:49:01.440
it's not ancient Middle East or so.

00:49:01.440 --> 00:49:05.910
It's China that is the
most ancient country.

00:49:05.910 --> 00:49:06.873
And in it,

00:49:08.820 --> 00:49:11.460
he writes then at, "The
journal of the Chinese empire,

00:49:11.460 --> 00:49:14.520
the most authentic and useful
newspaper in the world,

00:49:14.520 --> 00:49:17.910
since it contains the details
of all the public needs,

00:49:17.910 --> 00:49:22.910
resources, and interest of
all the orders of the State."

00:49:23.400 --> 00:49:28.110
So he writes some other things,
but that's most important.

00:49:28.110 --> 00:49:31.710
Now besides the fact that
he mentions the gazette

00:49:31.710 --> 00:49:33.390
and that he admires the gazette,

00:49:33.390 --> 00:49:37.770
he also uses the information included

00:49:37.770 --> 00:49:41.197
in the text by Contancin and also

00:49:41.197 --> 00:49:42.690
"Description... de la Chine"

00:49:42.690 --> 00:49:47.523
to discover his own idea
of an ideal government.

00:49:48.360 --> 00:49:52.020
So for him, "The new emperor Yontching

00:49:52.020 --> 00:49:54.120
surpassed his father in the love

00:49:54.120 --> 00:49:56.160
of laws and public good."

00:49:56.160 --> 00:49:58.530
These are all key notions,

00:49:58.530 --> 00:50:02.730
the love of laws, that enters
in the French discussions,

00:50:02.730 --> 00:50:04.170
and the public good.

00:50:04.170 --> 00:50:07.140
And "No emperor encouraged
agriculture more."

00:50:07.140 --> 00:50:09.600
This is the physiocrat,

00:50:09.600 --> 00:50:14.600
will promoted the development
of agriculture for economy.

00:50:15.780 --> 00:50:18.993
So he admires the role of the emperor.

00:50:20.070 --> 00:50:22.620
There is no, in his eyes,

00:50:22.620 --> 00:50:26.640
there is no evidence of a
despotic or arbitrary government.

00:50:26.640 --> 00:50:28.020
It is a monarch,

00:50:28.020 --> 00:50:33.020
will governs relying on
subordinated administrative boards.

00:50:33.090 --> 00:50:36.990
And the officials were
selected through exams.

00:50:36.990 --> 00:50:39.140
That was, yeah, something...

00:50:42.120 --> 00:50:45.210
The Europeans were fascinated

00:50:45.210 --> 00:50:48.063
by the Chinese examination system.

00:50:49.320 --> 00:50:52.410
Which of course did not exist in France

00:50:52.410 --> 00:50:53.810
or in Germany or in England.

00:50:54.900 --> 00:50:57.780
And he also states that it's a lay state

00:50:57.780 --> 00:50:59.280
based on justice and merit,

00:50:59.280 --> 00:51:02.550
in which virtue is
independent from religion.

00:51:02.550 --> 00:51:05.347
That is one of his major statement

00:51:05.347 --> 00:51:07.650
"in which personal
value was more important

00:51:07.650 --> 00:51:11.190
than hereditary privileges."

00:51:11.190 --> 00:51:12.490
As was the case in France.

00:51:14.220 --> 00:51:17.130
So this is a first approach.

00:51:17.130 --> 00:51:22.130
Quite positive narrative
about the text that he finds,

00:51:24.270 --> 00:51:28.020
and proclaiming how government should be,

00:51:28.020 --> 00:51:33.020
and how France can take
inspiration from the Chinese case.

00:51:34.320 --> 00:51:39.300
Not all the enlightenment
thinkers had the same opinion.

00:51:39.300 --> 00:51:40.413
Montesquieu.

00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:44.310
Here we can also prove that he read text

00:51:44.310 --> 00:51:46.470
about the gazette,

00:51:46.470 --> 00:51:50.577
namely is annotations in
"Description... de la Chine."

00:51:51.450 --> 00:51:56.450
And he has a different opinion,
because according to him,

00:51:57.480 --> 00:52:01.650
it is a despotic state.

00:52:01.650 --> 00:52:05.250
It is a state with arbitrary power.

00:52:05.250 --> 00:52:08.640
And what is one of his arguments?

00:52:08.640 --> 00:52:12.247
He writes, "The author," namely Contancin,

00:52:12.247 --> 00:52:14.490
"praises the gazette of painting...

00:52:14.490 --> 00:52:17.310
Of Peking printed every day.

00:52:17.310 --> 00:52:19.770
Nothing is put in it that has not

00:52:19.770 --> 00:52:21.150
been presented to the Emperor."

00:52:21.150 --> 00:52:22.957
We have seen the sentences.

00:52:22.957 --> 00:52:26.250
"Two writers were punished with death

00:52:26.250 --> 00:52:28.650
by putting false circumstances.

00:52:28.650 --> 00:52:31.800
The judgment of the tribunal
was founded on the fact

00:52:31.800 --> 00:52:34.320
that they had disrespected the Emperor,

00:52:34.320 --> 00:52:36.750
which is a capital crime."

00:52:36.750 --> 00:52:41.437
And then comes the
sentence by Montesquieu,

00:52:41.437 --> 00:52:46.140
"Nothing is so dangerous
as these vague laws,

00:52:46.140 --> 00:52:49.887
the disrespect is an arbitrary thing."

00:52:51.090 --> 00:52:56.090
So he will interpret
descendants and he say,

00:52:56.377 --> 00:52:58.890
"Yeah, that's a very nice example

00:52:58.890 --> 00:53:00.960
of something that is very arbitrary.

00:53:00.960 --> 00:53:03.610
Who is deciding on whether this is

00:53:04.500 --> 00:53:07.830
not founded on facts or not?"

00:53:07.830 --> 00:53:12.660
And so he... His classification of China

00:53:12.660 --> 00:53:14.913
is a despotic state.

00:53:16.800 --> 00:53:21.540
Quesnay is an physiocrat,
and he has a text

00:53:21.540 --> 00:53:23.190
on "Despotisme de la Chine."

00:53:23.190 --> 00:53:25.800
But we will see that his
interpretation as is...

00:53:25.800 --> 00:53:30.800
Of despotism is different
from the one of Montesquieu.

00:53:31.500 --> 00:53:33.367
Because for him,

00:53:33.367 --> 00:53:38.367
"China is a country governed
by legitimate despots.

00:53:38.790 --> 00:53:42.570
This is not a barbarian government.

00:53:42.570 --> 00:53:45.570
Its fundamental Constitution was entirely

00:53:45.570 --> 00:53:48.750
independent from the emperor."

00:53:48.750 --> 00:53:53.100
So he explicitly makes
reference to the gazette.

00:53:53.100 --> 00:53:57.210
It is a daily historical instruction.

00:53:57.210 --> 00:54:02.043
And we find, again, the
case of the two writers.

00:54:03.330 --> 00:54:06.450
In his eyes, "The law in China permits

00:54:06.450 --> 00:54:09.570
the practice of
remonstration of the emperor.

00:54:09.570 --> 00:54:13.950
There is perhaps no
country where the sovereign

00:54:13.950 --> 00:54:17.907
can be remonstrated with
more liberty than in China."

00:54:18.810 --> 00:54:21.213
So that's the interpretation by Quesnay.

00:54:23.190 --> 00:54:24.150
The final case.

00:54:24.150 --> 00:54:27.360
Now, these were three
enlightenment thinkers,

00:54:27.360 --> 00:54:29.280
but at the same time,

00:54:29.280 --> 00:54:32.100
we have another very interesting case.

00:54:32.100 --> 00:54:34.090
Namely the first

00:54:36.660 --> 00:54:41.040
Freedom of Press and
Freedom of Writing Act

00:54:41.040 --> 00:54:43.050
in the whole of Europe,

00:54:43.050 --> 00:54:48.050
which was voted in the
Swedish Parliament in 1766.

00:54:50.190 --> 00:54:52.080
Now, Lena Rydholm,

00:54:52.080 --> 00:54:56.940
and I could quote the
text also in my own book,

00:54:56.940 --> 00:55:01.940
has shown in detail how the
two members of parliament

00:55:03.570 --> 00:55:08.093
during several years
promoted this press act.

00:55:10.080 --> 00:55:12.660
And finally, it was approved.

00:55:12.660 --> 00:55:15.720
What was one of their sources

00:55:15.720 --> 00:55:19.023
was "Description... de la Chine"

00:55:19.980 --> 00:55:22.293
and the reference to the gazette.

00:55:24.870 --> 00:55:27.533
She writes, "the freedom
of writing, of the press,"

00:55:27.533 --> 00:55:30.457
and you can see that in the text.

00:55:30.457 --> 00:55:33.420
"And the information had been in existence

00:55:33.420 --> 00:55:35.520
in China since ancient times,

00:55:35.520 --> 00:55:37.770
and had contributed substantially

00:55:37.770 --> 00:55:40.260
to the wealth and stability of China."

00:55:40.260 --> 00:55:43.147
In other words, in Europe, one said,

00:55:43.147 --> 00:55:46.470
"We need a Freedom of Press Act,

00:55:46.470 --> 00:55:49.530
and our big example is China."

00:55:49.530 --> 00:55:54.060
Because there, since long
times, the freedom of press

00:55:54.060 --> 00:55:57.003
has contributed to wealth and stability.

00:55:58.980 --> 00:56:03.980
So to summarize this
part on the reception,

00:56:05.017 --> 00:56:09.240
"Enlightenment thinkers based
themself on the information

00:56:09.240 --> 00:56:11.160
from the gazettes."

00:56:11.160 --> 00:56:15.090
And they developed their own ideas on it.

00:56:15.090 --> 00:56:17.280
We have seen Voltaire and Quesnay.

00:56:17.280 --> 00:56:19.650
Of course, I'm very fast
in the presentation.

00:56:19.650 --> 00:56:22.170
These are general
presentations in the text.

00:56:22.170 --> 00:56:24.690
Of course, it's much more subtle.

00:56:24.690 --> 00:56:28.140
It's quite positive.
Montesquieu is negative.

00:56:28.140 --> 00:56:33.140
And the two Swedish members of parliament

00:56:33.360 --> 00:56:35.943
used it to promote the
freedom of the Press.

00:56:37.080 --> 00:56:41.497
Osterhammel, another quotation
I like that he mentions,

00:56:41.497 --> 00:56:43.830
"The objection that China was idealized

00:56:43.830 --> 00:56:46.380
in "Sinophile" texts,

00:56:46.380 --> 00:56:48.990
which drew the information mainly from

00:56:48.990 --> 00:56:51.030
'Description... de la
Chine,' may be correct,

00:56:51.030 --> 00:56:53.070
but misses the point."

00:56:53.070 --> 00:56:56.490
Because first, "This Chinese bureaucracy

00:56:56.490 --> 00:56:58.320
in the first half of the 18th century

00:56:58.320 --> 00:57:02.250
was indeed perhaps the best
functioning in the world.

00:57:02.250 --> 00:57:05.640
And its idealization was
an extrapolation from,

00:57:05.640 --> 00:57:07.470
yeah, a certain reality."

00:57:07.470 --> 00:57:10.230
Because Europe, even until today,

00:57:10.230 --> 00:57:11.763
does not have such a system.

00:57:13.140 --> 00:57:16.740
And second, "Reports
from the Middle Kingdom

00:57:16.740 --> 00:57:20.550
gave European theorists
the first opportunity

00:57:20.550 --> 00:57:23.160
to come to grips in a detailed way

00:57:23.160 --> 00:57:26.250
with the problems of
administration and governance."

00:57:26.250 --> 00:57:29.730
And you see how it functioned here.

00:57:29.730 --> 00:57:34.730
So here, they are no longer
investigating the gazette.

00:57:35.610 --> 00:57:37.890
They are not using it as a means

00:57:37.890 --> 00:57:40.140
to understand why there is a gazette.

00:57:40.140 --> 00:57:44.520
But the gazette is kind of
mirror, eh, that they use.

00:57:44.520 --> 00:57:49.170
I would say not in the first
place to understand China,

00:57:49.170 --> 00:57:52.500
but to develop their own ideas.

00:57:52.500 --> 00:57:57.500
That's the interaction
that happened at that time.

00:57:57.570 --> 00:58:01.020
And for us, or let's say for this topic,

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:04.560
what is interesting is
that this all happened

00:58:04.560 --> 00:58:06.420
by the fact that there
were some missionaries

00:58:06.420 --> 00:58:08.850
who decided that maybe this information

00:58:08.850 --> 00:58:11.790
is important to translate
and send to Europe.

00:58:11.790 --> 00:58:15.363
They never foresaw what
happened with this information.

00:58:16.560 --> 00:58:18.060
Now, very briefly,

00:58:18.060 --> 00:58:20.970
just to give a taste of what still

00:58:20.970 --> 00:58:25.970
can be read in the book
is the Qianlong period.

00:58:27.450 --> 00:58:30.194
There you have a major source,

00:58:30.194 --> 00:58:32.312
"Memoires concernant
L'Histoire, Les Sciences,

00:58:32.312 --> 00:58:35.495
Les Art, Les Moeurs, Les
Usages, Des Chinois."

00:58:35.495 --> 00:58:38.400
15 volumes, and then two volumes

00:58:38.400 --> 00:58:42.000
after the French Revolution
about 30 years later...

00:58:42.000 --> 00:58:43.203
20 years later.

00:58:44.250 --> 00:58:48.903
Very impressive text with
plenty of translations.

00:58:50.550 --> 00:58:54.180
And this was the result of an interaction,

00:58:54.180 --> 00:58:56.520
or an initiative, by Henri Bertin,

00:58:56.520 --> 00:58:59.700
who was the counselor of the French King.

00:58:59.700 --> 00:59:03.690
And who was, for 30 years,

00:59:03.690 --> 00:59:07.710
in correspondence with
missionaries in Beijing.

00:59:07.710 --> 00:59:10.830
And I mention here Amiot
and also Bourgeois.

00:59:10.830 --> 00:59:13.320
We come back on him.

00:59:13.320 --> 00:59:15.083
You have the picture of Amiot.

00:59:16.590 --> 00:59:18.780
And we should not forget,

00:59:18.780 --> 00:59:21.510
that's very important to underscore,

00:59:21.510 --> 00:59:25.800
that all these missionaries
work together with Chinese.

00:59:25.800 --> 00:59:29.340
This was always interaction,
a collaboration.

00:59:29.340 --> 00:59:34.340
And in the case of Amiot, we
know the name of this Chinese.

00:59:34.800 --> 00:59:35.940
And when he died,

00:59:35.940 --> 00:59:40.380
it was kind of biography
and commemoration of him.

00:59:40.380 --> 00:59:43.140
Also in the remark (speaks French)

00:59:43.140 --> 00:59:47.997
What is also important
is that in the 1760s?

00:59:51.840 --> 00:59:56.430
Yeah, 1760s, there were
at least two Chinese

00:59:56.430 --> 01:00:01.430
who spent, I think, eight or
nine years in France, in Paris.

01:00:02.910 --> 01:00:05.670
They entered Jesuit society there,

01:00:05.670 --> 01:00:07.650
but then the Jesuits were abolished,

01:00:07.650 --> 01:00:09.570
but they remained priests.

01:00:09.570 --> 01:00:14.040
And when they returned, or
wanted to return, to China,

01:00:14.040 --> 01:00:18.930
Bertin told them, "Well
I pay you for one year

01:00:18.930 --> 01:00:23.250
to travel around in
France and to go and visit

01:00:23.250 --> 01:00:26.610
factories for porcelain and for goblin,"

01:00:26.610 --> 01:00:28.563
and so on and so on.

01:00:28.563 --> 01:00:30.750
"And to discover the economy

01:00:30.750 --> 01:00:33.720
and the cultural aspect in France.

01:00:33.720 --> 01:00:35.820
And then when you go back to China,

01:00:35.820 --> 01:00:40.820
you report about the same
topics back to Europe."

01:00:41.160 --> 01:00:45.150
And so these people, these two Chinese,

01:00:45.150 --> 01:00:48.183
they have also plenty of text in French.

01:00:49.020 --> 01:00:50.170
in the (speaks French).

01:00:51.630 --> 01:00:55.800
Now in it, you have
also a few hundred pages

01:00:55.800 --> 01:00:57.060
only on the gazette.

01:00:57.060 --> 01:00:58.350
And I select this one,

01:00:58.350 --> 01:01:02.433
because there is three reference
to Sie-kou-tfiuen-chou.

01:01:03.480 --> 01:01:08.480
So in 19... In 1779, we
have extensive reports

01:01:10.920 --> 01:01:12.920
on the existence of the (speaks French).

01:01:25.468 --> 01:01:28.560
So... And details the description of it.

01:01:28.560 --> 01:01:30.740
And the...

01:01:34.394 --> 01:01:37.470
(speaks Mandarin) that I showed before.

01:01:37.470 --> 01:01:41.360
Are the original because
at a certain moment...

01:01:42.411 --> 01:01:44.070
"I will send you a few copies."

01:01:44.070 --> 01:01:47.250
And so that's why we have them in Paris.

01:01:47.250 --> 01:01:49.410
And you have the detailed discussions

01:01:49.410 --> 01:01:54.030
on the Qianlong emperor,

01:01:54.030 --> 01:01:58.770
correcting or punishing some of his sons

01:01:58.770 --> 01:02:03.330
because they made mistakes
in the transcription.

01:02:03.330 --> 01:02:08.330
Because the emperor
regularly called some volumes

01:02:08.730 --> 01:02:11.337
that were transcribed
of the (speaks French).

01:02:12.240 --> 01:02:15.090
And then, he tried to find some mistakes,

01:02:15.090 --> 01:02:17.497
and then he sent it back and he said,

01:02:17.497 --> 01:02:18.528
"Well, here.

01:02:18.528 --> 01:02:20.460
And there is a whole system of punishment

01:02:20.460 --> 01:02:24.447
for every character
that you wrote wrongly."

01:02:26.070 --> 01:02:27.900
Now, what happened with this,

01:02:27.900 --> 01:02:29.973
we have also the notes by Bertin.

01:02:31.500 --> 01:02:34.770
He was in regular contact
with the French king.

01:02:34.770 --> 01:02:37.370
And here you have the quote...

01:02:41.190 --> 01:02:42.513
I have to see it here.

01:02:45.867 --> 01:02:48.534
(speaks French)

01:02:57.624 --> 01:02:59.880
He has read, and that's
a passage that refers

01:02:59.880 --> 01:03:02.157
also to the "Siku quanshu."

01:03:03.000 --> 01:03:05.040
So we know that the
French king was informed

01:03:05.040 --> 01:03:06.060
about the "Siku quanshu."

01:03:06.060 --> 01:03:08.873
but what is even more is that Bertin

01:03:09.840 --> 01:03:13.707
wanted to acquire a copy
of the "Siku quanshu."

01:03:14.670 --> 01:03:18.967
So here he writes to China,

01:03:18.967 --> 01:03:21.420
"This collection would
be a superb presence

01:03:21.420 --> 01:03:24.990
to make to our Europe
which could thus enjoy

01:03:24.990 --> 01:03:26.760
all the Chinese literature.

01:03:26.760 --> 01:03:30.630
Please let me know if it would
be impossible to acquire it,

01:03:30.630 --> 01:03:33.870
transmitted and packaged
at Canton, for example,

01:03:33.870 --> 01:03:36.870
by making some gifts of
price to the Emperor,

01:03:36.870 --> 01:03:38.670
which may be agreeable to him,

01:03:38.670 --> 01:03:41.697
for example, tapestries,
goblins, mirrors."

01:03:42.930 --> 01:03:46.350
So it's a very nice case how an advisor

01:03:46.350 --> 01:03:51.350
to the French King in the 18th century

01:03:51.840 --> 01:03:56.840
informed about this major
book project in China.

01:03:57.450 --> 01:04:00.810
And even though it did not
know one character of Chinese,

01:04:00.810 --> 01:04:02.880
wanted to acquire it.

01:04:02.880 --> 01:04:04.470
Where are the times?

01:04:04.470 --> 01:04:06.570
Where in Europe today?

01:04:06.570 --> 01:04:11.570
There is a president or
king who gets the advice.

01:04:12.817 --> 01:04:15.570
"Please, can we have a Chinese database

01:04:15.570 --> 01:04:18.120
because that's so important.

01:04:18.120 --> 01:04:21.177
We are very far from that times."

01:04:22.770 --> 01:04:25.168
Just to finish with this here.

01:04:25.168 --> 01:04:27.480
Bertin was also in contact with others,

01:04:27.480 --> 01:04:30.690
like, for instance, Francois Bourgeois.

01:04:30.690 --> 01:04:32.137
And Francois Bourgeois said,

01:04:32.137 --> 01:04:34.890
"Well I will explain
you what is a gazette.

01:04:34.890 --> 01:04:37.650
I will translate you
three months of gazette."

01:04:37.650 --> 01:04:40.530
And so you have a whole manuscript

01:04:40.530 --> 01:04:44.100
with translation of
nearly two or three months

01:04:44.100 --> 01:04:46.770
of the gazette from the
beginning to the end.

01:04:46.770 --> 01:04:49.680
It only leaves out the name list

01:04:49.680 --> 01:04:52.440
of all the people who were appointed.

01:04:52.440 --> 01:04:55.590
You can find that in the (speaks French).

01:04:55.590 --> 01:04:57.120
It was not published.

01:04:57.120 --> 01:05:00.180
But again, it shows how much information

01:05:00.180 --> 01:05:03.210
about the gazette that was sent to Europe.

01:05:03.210 --> 01:05:06.720
And in it you find all kind
of interesting information

01:05:06.720 --> 01:05:09.870
among others about this
Lin Shuangwen rebellion

01:05:09.870 --> 01:05:12.630
in Taiwan that took place.

01:05:12.630 --> 01:05:14.730
And you have a whole discussion

01:05:14.730 --> 01:05:17.167
because Francois Bourgeois will say,

01:05:17.167 --> 01:05:20.100
"Yes, but they are saying that the...

01:05:20.100 --> 01:05:23.190
That's the information by
the military officials,

01:05:23.190 --> 01:05:25.710
but in fact we have other information."

01:05:25.710 --> 01:05:28.350
And so that's an interesting case

01:05:28.350 --> 01:05:29.800
of distribution of

01:05:32.280 --> 01:05:33.213
information.

01:05:34.470 --> 01:05:37.170
I come to a conclusion.

01:05:37.170 --> 01:05:41.790
So I wanted, on the basis of the gazette,

01:05:41.790 --> 01:05:44.640
which really started, as I
mentioned in the beginning,

01:05:44.640 --> 01:05:47.040
as a kind of mistake because that was not

01:05:47.040 --> 01:05:50.687
my intention to spend three
years on the the gazettes.

01:05:50.687 --> 01:05:54.300
But... And when people ask
me, "What are you doing?"

01:05:54.300 --> 01:05:56.223
I say, "I'm reading the gazette."

01:05:58.521 --> 01:06:02.417
So first, I hope to have shown you

01:06:03.330 --> 01:06:06.870
that by looking at this outside, again,

01:06:06.870 --> 01:06:09.810
it's not the only way to write history.

01:06:09.810 --> 01:06:14.160
That also this European gazette's sources

01:06:14.160 --> 01:06:18.330
can expand the knowledge
about what was the gazette

01:06:18.330 --> 01:06:19.653
in the 18th century.

01:06:20.910 --> 01:06:22.740
And that this is also a nice case

01:06:22.740 --> 01:06:25.380
of this interconnected history.

01:06:25.380 --> 01:06:28.320
Not only by the fact that
they write about the gazette,

01:06:28.320 --> 01:06:30.840
how they used the gazette as Europeans,

01:06:30.840 --> 01:06:33.420
but also how it circulated in Europe.

01:06:33.420 --> 01:06:38.420
And also was an inspiration
for a number of people

01:06:38.460 --> 01:06:42.060
for development of their own ideas.

01:06:42.060 --> 01:06:43.470
Not only about China,

01:06:43.470 --> 01:06:46.533
but mainly about political
ideas for Europe.

01:06:47.700 --> 01:06:51.630
So we can conclude that
already in the 18th century,

01:06:51.630 --> 01:06:53.610
the Chinese gazette, yeah,

01:06:53.610 --> 01:06:58.590
joined, in one way or
another, global public.

01:06:58.590 --> 01:07:01.200
I thank you for your attention.

01:07:01.200 --> 01:07:04.367
(audience applauding)

01:07:07.420 --> 01:07:11.013
- (speaks softly) questions now.

01:07:23.130 --> 01:07:24.570
- [Student] Thank you very much for the

01:07:24.570 --> 01:07:26.310
very informative presentation.

01:07:26.310 --> 01:07:29.580
My question is, during the
course of your research,

01:07:29.580 --> 01:07:30.720
did you happen to...

01:07:30.720 --> 01:07:33.090
Could you say more about
the printing process,

01:07:33.090 --> 01:07:36.390
because the locus of my
inquiries, based on this premise,

01:07:36.390 --> 01:07:38.850
that if there was a freedom oppressed,

01:07:38.850 --> 01:07:41.700
then were there any
private presses that exist

01:07:41.700 --> 01:07:44.640
during that time period? Thank you.

01:07:44.640 --> 01:07:47.280
- Well, I would not join completely

01:07:47.280 --> 01:07:49.510
the freedom of press

01:07:51.060 --> 01:07:54.000
in the sense that what is said there

01:07:54.000 --> 01:07:57.553
about the freedom of press
and the circulation of,

01:07:59.550 --> 01:08:01.413
let's say, of information.

01:08:02.370 --> 01:08:07.370
As you can see what I was
talking about is the gazette,

01:08:08.190 --> 01:08:13.190
which is, to a large extent,
an official document.

01:08:15.270 --> 01:08:18.480
And you have the, let's say,

01:08:18.480 --> 01:08:21.690
the decisions that are
taken in the palace.

01:08:21.690 --> 01:08:24.240
On the basis of this decision,

01:08:24.240 --> 01:08:28.740
they make a selection of
what would be made public

01:08:28.740 --> 01:08:30.330
and for different reasons,

01:08:30.330 --> 01:08:32.430
because they think that the...

01:08:32.430 --> 01:08:35.310
It's important that the decision is known,

01:08:35.310 --> 01:08:38.790
but also that it's true
as a kind of instruction,

01:08:38.790 --> 01:08:43.790
because it can have
precedent in decision making.

01:08:44.010 --> 01:08:49.010
And then, this was copied,
and then it was printed.

01:08:49.770 --> 01:08:52.266
And then in terms of printing technique,

01:08:52.266 --> 01:08:55.320
these were mobile characters.

01:08:55.320 --> 01:08:57.750
And for some, there is a whole discussion.

01:08:57.750 --> 01:09:02.750
Was it mobile characters in
wood or the (speaks Mandarin)

01:09:05.310 --> 01:09:09.150
For some scholars, this was in wax.

01:09:09.150 --> 01:09:13.470
In wax where you can carve quicker.

01:09:13.470 --> 01:09:15.210
But then it's also a block.

01:09:15.210 --> 01:09:18.930
And then, this was sent to the provinces.

01:09:18.930 --> 01:09:23.930
And there, again, they were republished,

01:09:24.330 --> 01:09:25.530
or redistributed.

01:09:25.530 --> 01:09:29.370
And sometimes also by
hundred... Let's say hundred.

01:09:29.370 --> 01:09:32.490
So that is the system of this gazette.

01:09:32.490 --> 01:09:33.993
So this is not,

01:09:34.860 --> 01:09:39.510
I would say completely
a freedom of the press.

01:09:39.510 --> 01:09:42.150
What we understand by
freedom of the press.

01:09:42.150 --> 01:09:43.533
On the other hand,

01:09:44.550 --> 01:09:49.550
you had, in China, the
publications of books.

01:09:50.820 --> 01:09:53.880
And there was, unlike in Europe,

01:09:53.880 --> 01:09:58.443
there was no pre-printing censorship.

01:09:59.550 --> 01:10:03.963
In Europe, certainly in the
17th century, even later on,

01:10:04.860 --> 01:10:06.780
you wanted to publish a book,

01:10:06.780 --> 01:10:10.290
you needed the permission of the church

01:10:10.290 --> 01:10:13.263
and of the state and... Or of vote.

01:10:14.130 --> 01:10:18.060
And you see that in all the
text that it has mentioned.

01:10:18.060 --> 01:10:23.060
You have nothing really
similar to that in China.

01:10:23.070 --> 01:10:27.709
Of course there could be
post-printing prohibition

01:10:27.709 --> 01:10:29.340
of the text.

01:10:29.340 --> 01:10:33.663
So these are, in fact, different matters.

01:10:34.800 --> 01:10:39.390
But I quoted that because they...

01:10:39.390 --> 01:10:42.090
On the basis of the gazette,

01:10:42.090 --> 01:10:45.720
whether what they write about
the gazette is true or false,

01:10:45.720 --> 01:10:50.720
but this gave an inspiration
to develop an own idea.

01:10:51.462 --> 01:10:55.260
That's what we see what
happens with this knowledge.

01:10:55.260 --> 01:10:57.600
And that can be interpret
in different ways.

01:10:57.600 --> 01:11:00.810
We can say, "Well they
just fantasize about China,

01:11:00.810 --> 01:11:02.940
or they have seen all files."

01:11:02.940 --> 01:11:06.577
Or you can also say,
what I sometimes say is,

01:11:06.577 --> 01:11:10.500
"Yeah, but look how this
creates new knowledge

01:11:10.500 --> 01:11:14.160
that is, to a certain extent, disconnected

01:11:14.160 --> 01:11:15.960
from the original meaning."

01:11:15.960 --> 01:11:19.680
But that happens with so many
of the things that we invent

01:11:19.680 --> 01:11:23.223
or write, that this kind
of circulation happens.

01:11:24.390 --> 01:11:25.830
- [Student] Well thanks
for that, Nicholas.

01:11:25.830 --> 01:11:27.780
That was really great.

01:11:27.780 --> 01:11:29.730
And I laughed seeing the Voltaire quote

01:11:29.730 --> 01:11:31.320
where he talks about how everyone thinks

01:11:31.320 --> 01:11:35.040
the gazette is free and also true.

01:11:35.040 --> 01:11:36.570
Because in the 1620s,

01:11:36.570 --> 01:11:39.000
no Chinese reader or a missionary reader

01:11:39.000 --> 01:11:40.710
really trusted the gazettes-
- Yeah.

01:11:40.710 --> 01:11:42.300
- [Student] Because it was full of

01:11:42.300 --> 01:11:44.640
pre-imperial review material.
- Yeah.

01:11:44.640 --> 01:11:45.570
- [Student] Which gets to the point

01:11:45.570 --> 01:11:47.730
that this is a very dynamic genre

01:11:47.730 --> 01:11:50.760
that by the Yeongjong reign is also

01:11:50.760 --> 01:11:53.790
perfectly suited to now
re-representing the state

01:11:53.790 --> 01:11:57.240
as a form of sort of auto propaganda,

01:11:57.240 --> 01:12:01.770
even as the contents are true
and useful to governance.

01:12:01.770 --> 01:12:04.980
And so my question for
you is to think about

01:12:04.980 --> 01:12:06.510
what the actual function of the gazette

01:12:06.510 --> 01:12:08.193
is for European readers?

01:12:09.450 --> 01:12:12.030
When the producer sees this as a tool

01:12:12.030 --> 01:12:14.730
that also produces propaganda,

01:12:14.730 --> 01:12:17.910
whereas in Europe, they're
actually reflecting back

01:12:17.910 --> 01:12:20.070
that propaganda almost perfectly.

01:12:20.070 --> 01:12:22.770
So how should we understand early modern

01:12:22.770 --> 01:12:27.180
global power relations if
we, in this broader context,

01:12:27.180 --> 01:12:30.090
think about the ways
Europeans were consuming

01:12:30.090 --> 01:12:32.940
Chinese propaganda and the
Chinese vision of itself,

01:12:32.940 --> 01:12:36.090
which we've retrospectively
called Sinophilia,

01:12:36.090 --> 01:12:38.550
when in fact that's just
the way Chinese texts

01:12:38.550 --> 01:12:40.534
talk about themselves too?

01:12:40.534 --> 01:12:41.790
- Yeah, yeah.

01:12:41.790 --> 01:12:45.510
Yeah, you have a very
interesting and broad question.

01:12:45.510 --> 01:12:47.907
So first, under the Yeongjong...

01:12:47.907 --> 01:12:51.570
And that's why I think
Perroni is interesting,

01:12:51.570 --> 01:12:54.720
because he's a bit more critical.

01:12:54.720 --> 01:12:59.100
Because he would say and
give very concrete examples.

01:12:59.100 --> 01:13:03.637
There is a flood
somewhere, and he will say,

01:13:03.637 --> 01:13:07.440
"Yeah, that is reported by the officials,

01:13:07.440 --> 01:13:10.320
but that does not represent
the reality." Yeah?

01:13:10.320 --> 01:13:15.150
So you see the conflict that is there,

01:13:15.150 --> 01:13:17.490
that this is an interpretation,

01:13:17.490 --> 01:13:19.530
and that this is not just...

01:13:19.530 --> 01:13:22.593
Or let's say that it is
propaganda by the state,

01:13:24.270 --> 01:13:27.903
but you have Perroni's more
articulated view on it.

01:13:28.920 --> 01:13:31.830
Unfortunately, it was not Perroni's text

01:13:31.830 --> 01:13:33.420
that were published.

01:13:33.420 --> 01:13:36.280
And so what was published is

01:13:38.160 --> 01:13:39.903
Contoncin's vision.

01:13:41.370 --> 01:13:45.530
And indeed, there you have the...

01:13:47.610 --> 01:13:51.390
Criticism that the
missionaries give two words,

01:13:51.390 --> 01:13:54.600
the newspapers in Europe,

01:13:54.600 --> 01:13:57.750
and that then they contrast with China

01:13:57.750 --> 01:14:00.930
where everything seems to be beautiful,

01:14:00.930 --> 01:14:05.280
because finally you have
something that is official

01:14:05.280 --> 01:14:06.900
in which they trust.

01:14:06.900 --> 01:14:09.990
And that can be used for instruction

01:14:09.990 --> 01:14:11.823
information of officials.

01:14:13.530 --> 01:14:16.470
And the way in which the people,

01:14:16.470 --> 01:14:18.600
let's say, thinkers reacted on that.

01:14:18.600 --> 01:14:22.053
There you have again, a
wide variety of reactions.

01:14:22.950 --> 01:14:24.990
So, but it's, in a certain way,

01:14:24.990 --> 01:14:29.193
it's suited some of the
opinions in Europe also.

01:14:30.150 --> 01:14:31.600
- [Student] Thank you. Sorry.

01:14:33.030 --> 01:14:36.540
The Hume is an occasion for
us to think about the past

01:14:36.540 --> 01:14:37.920
and the history of Sinology.

01:14:37.920 --> 01:14:40.290
And listening to you speak,

01:14:40.290 --> 01:14:42.270
I was of course thinking
about Jonathan Spence

01:14:42.270 --> 01:14:44.703
and how much he would've
enjoyed your lecture.

01:14:45.690 --> 01:14:47.070
I do have one question for you,

01:14:47.070 --> 01:14:50.643
which is not knowing anything
at all about the gazette.

01:14:52.380 --> 01:14:53.640
Your...

01:14:53.640 --> 01:14:56.640
You've found all this
information in the 18th century.

01:14:56.640 --> 01:14:58.890
Does that information change

01:14:58.890 --> 01:15:00.723
our overall view of the gazette?

01:15:04.110 --> 01:15:07.143
Going through to the 19th century?

01:15:09.870 --> 01:15:13.860
- [Nicolas] I do not
really dare to say too much

01:15:13.860 --> 01:15:17.460
because in the end, yeah, I was...

01:15:17.460 --> 01:15:21.510
I didn't want to write a book
on the gazette as a whole.

01:15:21.510 --> 01:15:22.950
And you have, for instance,

01:15:22.950 --> 01:15:27.950
the book by (indistinct) who
has the much broader view.

01:15:28.230 --> 01:15:31.770
So I didn't want to enter completely

01:15:31.770 --> 01:15:34.983
into that kind of discussion,

01:15:35.880 --> 01:15:40.687
but there are certainly
differences with the 19th century.

01:15:42.450 --> 01:15:46.953
Because the circulation of the
gazette is much more widely.

01:15:49.230 --> 01:15:51.060
This is one of the aspects.

01:15:51.060 --> 01:15:55.380
But also, yeah, what was the circulation

01:15:55.380 --> 01:15:58.920
in the 17th century also?

01:15:58.920 --> 01:16:02.370
We were discussing that earlier.

01:16:02.370 --> 01:16:07.370
Yeah we know relatively little
about the (speaks Mandarin)

01:16:10.920 --> 01:16:15.920
But when I look at the
missionary sources, yeah,

01:16:16.740 --> 01:16:19.070
you are quite...

01:16:21.150 --> 01:16:26.150
Convinced that they really
used these kind of sources.

01:16:26.430 --> 01:16:28.950
Because when they write and they...

01:16:28.950 --> 01:16:31.650
Every year they write an annual letter.

01:16:31.650 --> 01:16:36.030
And they write first, always
in the beginning of the letter,

01:16:36.030 --> 01:16:38.760
a state of the art of the country.

01:16:38.760 --> 01:16:40.620
And then, they have...

01:16:40.620 --> 01:16:42.000
And then Christianity.

01:16:42.000 --> 01:16:44.310
And then they have the provinces

01:16:44.310 --> 01:16:46.410
and something about the province,

01:16:46.410 --> 01:16:48.630
and then Christianity in the province.

01:16:48.630 --> 01:16:50.610
Now, when you look at
all this information,

01:16:50.610 --> 01:16:53.520
you simply wonder where did they got it?

01:16:53.520 --> 01:16:57.480
This was not, let's say,
was not oral information.

01:16:57.480 --> 01:17:01.380
It's so detailed that
it must have been based

01:17:01.380 --> 01:17:03.660
on these sources.

01:17:03.660 --> 01:17:05.970
But they were also digested

01:17:05.970 --> 01:17:08.313
because they make a kind of synthesis.

01:17:11.310 --> 01:17:14.280
But yeah, the structure...

01:17:14.280 --> 01:17:17.850
At least the structure
of the gazette changes,

01:17:17.850 --> 01:17:22.020
to a certain extent,
also in the 19th century.

01:17:22.020 --> 01:17:27.020
And you see some differences
in the structure of it.

01:17:27.897 --> 01:17:31.410
But I didn't want to get involved too much

01:17:31.410 --> 01:17:35.433
in the 19th century because
that's not my field.

01:17:36.390 --> 01:17:38.390
Yeah, thank you for your question. Yeah.

01:17:39.480 --> 01:17:41.370
- [Student] Thank you very
much. I really loved this talk.

01:17:41.370 --> 01:17:43.420
I just learned a lot. It was so much fun.

01:17:44.580 --> 01:17:46.140
So I know very little about this period,

01:17:46.140 --> 01:17:47.640
but I do know that this wasn't the only...

01:17:47.640 --> 01:17:49.500
These weren't the only
translations that were happening

01:17:49.500 --> 01:17:50.333
in this period. Right?

01:17:50.333 --> 01:17:51.840
And it might even be fair to say that

01:17:51.840 --> 01:17:54.510
the first wave of
translations of Chinese texts

01:17:54.510 --> 01:17:57.480
into Europe were translations
of the Chinese classics,

01:17:57.480 --> 01:17:59.310
of the four books and things like that

01:17:59.310 --> 01:18:00.560
maybe just a little bit
earlier in this period.

01:18:00.560 --> 01:18:02.490
So I'm wondering, could
you take a step back

01:18:02.490 --> 01:18:06.540
and just reflect on the
place of these translations

01:18:06.540 --> 01:18:09.540
of gazettes compared within this larger

01:18:09.540 --> 01:18:11.277
universe of translations of Chinese texts?

01:18:11.277 --> 01:18:12.780
Are there certain kinds of audiences

01:18:12.780 --> 01:18:14.187
that are more focused on the gazettes?

01:18:14.187 --> 01:18:16.500
Are there other kinds of audiences
that are more focused on,

01:18:16.500 --> 01:18:18.510
let's call it Confucius, stuff?

01:18:18.510 --> 01:18:20.790
Because that is maybe the
first wave of translations.

01:18:20.790 --> 01:18:23.160
Is there a competition in Europe

01:18:23.160 --> 01:18:24.930
to sort of to decide, you know,

01:18:24.930 --> 01:18:26.610
which sorts of texts are gonna determine

01:18:26.610 --> 01:18:28.260
really the image of China?

01:18:28.260 --> 01:18:30.150
Who are the players or their audiences

01:18:30.150 --> 01:18:30.983
and sub audiences?

01:18:30.983 --> 01:18:33.150
Like, what is the larger scene,

01:18:33.150 --> 01:18:35.910
and how does this talk fit
into that... Our sense of...

01:18:35.910 --> 01:18:40.910
- Yep, so first we can say
that the translations...

01:18:43.890 --> 01:18:48.770
Translations started
relatively late, because...

01:18:51.420 --> 01:18:52.800
Yeah, but a few exceptions.

01:18:52.800 --> 01:18:56.400
But you have the Confucius
philosophers, which is...

01:18:56.400 --> 01:18:59.883
So that's seven... 1689.

01:19:01.860 --> 01:19:06.180
Yeah, Matteo Ricci
arrived in China in 1582.

01:19:06.180 --> 01:19:08.973
So that's about 100 years later.

01:19:10.050 --> 01:19:12.090
So in which they make...

01:19:12.090 --> 01:19:14.373
And that's thanks to
the "exile" in Canton.

01:19:14.373 --> 01:19:15.360
They are together,

01:19:15.360 --> 01:19:19.530
and they really make a translation
of these basic classics.

01:19:19.530 --> 01:19:22.920
Now this...

01:19:22.920 --> 01:19:26.730
The time, let's say shift,

01:19:26.730 --> 01:19:30.480
is 1688,

01:19:30.480 --> 01:19:34.170
because (indistinct) comes back to Europe,

01:19:34.170 --> 01:19:37.140
who edited the (speaks French)

01:19:37.140 --> 01:19:38.763
And he goes to Paris,

01:19:39.630 --> 01:19:44.040
and he meets the people
of the (speaks French)

01:19:47.430 --> 01:19:50.797
And there this academicians say,

01:19:50.797 --> 01:19:54.480
"We need more information about China."

01:19:54.480 --> 01:19:59.310
And they have this enormous
list of what they want to know.

01:19:59.310 --> 01:20:00.990
What about history?

01:20:00.990 --> 01:20:04.380
What about the origin of the characters?

01:20:04.380 --> 01:20:08.433
What about zoology, botany,
all these questions.

01:20:09.390 --> 01:20:14.370
And so then, the French
missionaries will go to China.

01:20:14.370 --> 01:20:17.760
They are not sent by Rome,
not through Portugal,

01:20:17.760 --> 01:20:19.830
but from France.

01:20:19.830 --> 01:20:21.660
And they go to China.

01:20:21.660 --> 01:20:23.490
And there starts, yeah...

01:20:23.490 --> 01:20:28.380
This is shift from the
Renaissance to more, yeah,

01:20:28.380 --> 01:20:31.980
pre-modernity scholars.

01:20:31.980 --> 01:20:35.250
And in the beginning, they
are active missionaries,

01:20:35.250 --> 01:20:37.560
and they start to do some translations.

01:20:37.560 --> 01:20:42.560
But then in 1724, basically
you have an expulsion,

01:20:43.740 --> 01:20:47.730
and they are confined in Beijing.

01:20:47.730 --> 01:20:50.580
And they will be less...

01:20:50.580 --> 01:20:52.830
It will be less possible to produce,

01:20:52.830 --> 01:20:57.450
like Ricci and all these
people, text in Chinese.

01:20:57.450 --> 01:20:59.940
And there you see a shift also through

01:20:59.940 --> 01:21:01.710
the better communication,

01:21:01.710 --> 01:21:06.710
because the travel of
documents and so is less long

01:21:06.720 --> 01:21:11.720
where there is this, yeah,
still under (speaks French)

01:21:14.190 --> 01:21:18.270
Petersburg, Beijing, and in Paris.

01:21:18.270 --> 01:21:19.350
And they correspond,

01:21:19.350 --> 01:21:22.470
and they start to discuss chronology...

01:21:22.470 --> 01:21:26.730
Or they continue to discuss chronology

01:21:26.730 --> 01:21:28.877
and botany and so on and so on.

01:21:28.877 --> 01:21:31.980
And so from that time onwards,

01:21:31.980 --> 01:21:35.790
you have a kind of systematic attempt

01:21:35.790 --> 01:21:39.090
to solve all the questions
that they got there.

01:21:39.090 --> 01:21:40.770
And that is a shift,

01:21:40.770 --> 01:21:45.770
because the efforts of these
missionaries and translations

01:21:46.230 --> 01:21:50.970
will be more devoted to
translating Chinese text

01:21:50.970 --> 01:21:54.573
to Europe than European text to Chinese.

01:21:55.560 --> 01:22:00.480
And was there... Was
that adapted to republic?

01:22:00.480 --> 01:22:02.700
That was partly adapted to republic,

01:22:02.700 --> 01:22:05.400
but in the first place of academicians.

01:22:05.400 --> 01:22:09.390
So they were in contact
some with astronomy

01:22:09.390 --> 01:22:11.675
and the chronology, but yeah.

01:22:11.675 --> 01:22:15.510
(speaks French) that is botany and zoology

01:22:15.510 --> 01:22:20.510
and all these kind of texts.

01:22:21.060 --> 01:22:25.380
And then, you have the major
efforts by Du Halde, in fact,

01:22:25.380 --> 01:22:27.390
who says, "Yes, send me...

01:22:27.390 --> 01:22:32.390
I don't have anything
about moral philosophy."

01:22:33.120 --> 01:22:36.600
And then, he has a text about
moral philosophy and so on.

01:22:36.600 --> 01:22:39.120
So it becomes a major enterprise,

01:22:39.120 --> 01:22:41.160
which is still striking

01:22:41.160 --> 01:22:43.680
because if you simply look at the...

01:22:43.680 --> 01:22:45.693
We have it on our database also.

01:22:46.530 --> 01:22:48.150
All the text that were translated.

01:22:48.150 --> 01:22:49.860
Many of these text are still...

01:22:49.860 --> 01:22:53.130
We don't have a modern English, French,

01:22:53.130 --> 01:22:56.280
German, or whatever
translation of all these texts,

01:22:56.280 --> 01:23:01.280
which are original Chinese text
by very well-known authors,

01:23:01.440 --> 01:23:06.440
but also by rather unknown authors.

01:23:06.840 --> 01:23:11.730
Recently, you have in this
"Description... de la Chine."

01:23:11.730 --> 01:23:13.916
You have the book by (speaks French)

01:23:13.916 --> 01:23:17.100
who identified nearly all the text.

01:23:17.100 --> 01:23:18.510
There were five left,

01:23:18.510 --> 01:23:21.660
three were then identified
in the last few years.

01:23:21.660 --> 01:23:25.290
And I discovered now the last
two text on moral philosophy.

01:23:25.290 --> 01:23:27.447
And these are Chinese (speaks Mandarin),

01:23:28.770 --> 01:23:31.740
but by authors that we have never heard.

01:23:31.740 --> 01:23:33.750
And what is interesting is that then

01:23:33.750 --> 01:23:37.650
they illuminate the circulation
of certain text in China,

01:23:37.650 --> 01:23:41.520
because they were translated
and used by missionaries.

01:23:41.520 --> 01:23:45.540
Because how did it work?
It's relatively easy.

01:23:45.540 --> 01:23:47.610
You want to know something
about moral philosophy,

01:23:47.610 --> 01:23:49.957
you ask a Chinese scholar,

01:23:49.957 --> 01:23:52.140
"What do you... What is a good book?"

01:23:52.140 --> 01:23:55.467
And he says, "So in my
desk, I have this book here.

01:23:55.467 --> 01:23:56.760
You should translate this."

01:23:56.760 --> 01:23:59.283
And that's how it work. Yeah.

01:24:00.120 --> 01:24:01.710
- [Student] Thank you.

01:24:01.710 --> 01:24:06.710
As an anthropologist, I am
not tuned to changes in time.

01:24:08.820 --> 01:24:10.433
So I know I, you know,

01:24:10.433 --> 01:24:12.690
I would like to ask a question

01:24:12.690 --> 01:24:15.813
that's quite different from
my historian colleagues.

01:24:17.520 --> 01:24:21.450
I just want... You
know, your idea of China

01:24:21.450 --> 01:24:24.000
as a concept intrigues me.

01:24:24.000 --> 01:24:27.000
You know, I kind of like to have you

01:24:27.000 --> 01:24:29.580
to see if you can clarify your idea

01:24:29.580 --> 01:24:33.360
of how you define China
in that historical context

01:24:33.360 --> 01:24:37.440
and the missionary's perception of China.

01:24:37.440 --> 01:24:41.490
And then of course, the European scholars'

01:24:41.490 --> 01:24:44.670
concept of China and how they differ.

01:24:44.670 --> 01:24:46.440
'Cause I was also noticing that

01:24:46.440 --> 01:24:48.630
quite a bit of the activities

01:24:48.630 --> 01:24:50.613
were happening in Canton.

01:24:52.650 --> 01:24:56.910
At least, you know, in the
period you're treating.

01:24:56.910 --> 01:25:01.290
And I just wonder how
these people's conception

01:25:01.290 --> 01:25:06.290
of the country might be shaped
by very regional context

01:25:07.500 --> 01:25:09.630
that they found themselves in.

01:25:09.630 --> 01:25:12.510
So I just want to get
a sense of this kind of

01:25:12.510 --> 01:25:17.510
regional ethnic, you know...

01:25:17.587 --> 01:25:19.773
You know, ecologically even,

01:25:20.880 --> 01:25:24.300
diversity of this entity we call China,

01:25:24.300 --> 01:25:27.630
and how that affects the way that we would

01:25:27.630 --> 01:25:32.630
think about the gazettes and
then also their translations.

01:25:33.780 --> 01:25:34.950
- Yeah, thank you very much.

01:25:34.950 --> 01:25:37.780
It's also a very large question.

01:25:37.780 --> 01:25:40.170
I happened to write even articles

01:25:40.170 --> 01:25:43.890
about the concept of China in
all these missionary texts.

01:25:43.890 --> 01:25:47.640
But to be very brief, it is not China.

01:25:47.640 --> 01:25:50.730
It is Ming or it is Ching,

01:25:50.730 --> 01:25:52.320
and they adopt, in the first place,

01:25:52.320 --> 01:25:54.750
the auto definition by China.

01:25:54.750 --> 01:25:58.230
Because when you look at
Chinese maps of the time,

01:25:58.230 --> 01:25:59.763
they will never write China.

01:26:00.870 --> 01:26:04.650
Because if you have no authority...

01:26:04.650 --> 01:26:07.080
As a Belgium, if you
only have a Belgium map,

01:26:07.080 --> 01:26:08.910
you don't have to put Belgium.

01:26:08.910 --> 01:26:10.260
Because you only put Belgium

01:26:10.260 --> 01:26:13.173
when you have other countries around.

01:26:14.040 --> 01:26:16.710
What they will write is "Ming."

01:26:16.710 --> 01:26:18.030
Ming is a Ming Dynasty

01:26:18.030 --> 01:26:21.663
because your authority
is a former dynasty.

01:26:22.770 --> 01:26:25.350
And there it is Ching.

01:26:25.350 --> 01:26:30.134
Now the Ching is not
only what we call China

01:26:30.134 --> 01:26:31.350
(speaks Mandarin)

01:26:31.350 --> 01:26:32.970
it's also the Manchus.

01:26:32.970 --> 01:26:34.173
So in these texts...

01:26:34.173 --> 01:26:36.840
Well I didn't develop it, but in my book,

01:26:36.840 --> 01:26:39.060
I have a few pages on that.

01:26:39.060 --> 01:26:41.340
Because for the missionaries,

01:26:41.340 --> 01:26:43.770
when they communicate with the emperor,

01:26:43.770 --> 01:26:48.770
most often this is a
Manchu and not in Chinese.

01:26:49.050 --> 01:26:51.543
They use Manchu sources also.

01:26:52.620 --> 01:26:57.620
They even refer to some
possible Manchu gazettes. And...

01:27:00.570 --> 01:27:03.390
So that's one of the differentiations,

01:27:03.390 --> 01:27:05.290
ethnic differentiations, because yeah,

01:27:06.360 --> 01:27:07.193
these are the...

01:27:08.160 --> 01:27:11.820
The emperors, but the emperors are emperor

01:27:11.820 --> 01:27:15.810
of the Hun and the Mongols
and of the Tibetans

01:27:15.810 --> 01:27:20.810
and the Chinese and
the Manchus. So it's...

01:27:21.390 --> 01:27:25.110
There is already this
kind of "ethnic," if...

01:27:25.110 --> 01:27:27.690
We have to be prudent with all these words

01:27:27.690 --> 01:27:29.643
transforming at that time.

01:27:31.080 --> 01:27:34.360
And then... But that's less true for

01:27:35.310 --> 01:27:40.310
the information I have about, let's say,

01:27:42.312 --> 01:27:43.950
Amyu time.

01:27:43.950 --> 01:27:46.560
The missionaries are all only in Beijing.

01:27:46.560 --> 01:27:49.710
And that's, I would say, the direct link,

01:27:49.710 --> 01:27:53.940
because that's two kilometers
further it's produced,

01:27:53.940 --> 01:27:55.770
and you have your newspaper.

01:27:55.770 --> 01:27:57.570
In Canton, it's different

01:27:57.570 --> 01:28:00.420
because they get also
information from the provinces.

01:28:00.420 --> 01:28:05.420
So the provincial newspapers,
that's on the country period.

01:28:06.270 --> 01:28:08.730
There is a whole, yeah,

01:28:08.730 --> 01:28:12.510
the best information about
the Tangshan earthquake.

01:28:12.510 --> 01:28:15.450
It's by the missionaries and
not by the Chinese sources.

01:28:15.450 --> 01:28:18.420
And they based themself on the information

01:28:18.420 --> 01:28:20.850
from the different regions.

01:28:20.850 --> 01:28:23.400
So these are kind of intercessors

01:28:23.400 --> 01:28:26.080
where I would say, yeah, I really agree

01:28:27.150 --> 01:28:28.803
in this differentiation.

01:28:29.790 --> 01:28:31.680
But yeah, when you give a presentation,

01:28:31.680 --> 01:28:35.430
you cannot nuance every term.

01:28:35.430 --> 01:28:36.363
So yeah.

01:28:37.380 --> 01:28:38.700
- [Host] Thank you very much.

01:28:38.700 --> 01:28:42.210
We are continuing the conversation
upstairs in a reception,

01:28:42.210 --> 01:28:45.870
so feel free to come and ask
any further questions upstairs

01:28:45.870 --> 01:28:48.450
over a drink and something to eat.

01:28:48.450 --> 01:28:50.970
But I did want to thank
very much our speaker today

01:28:50.970 --> 01:28:54.240
for that really fascinating
and interesting talk.

01:28:54.240 --> 01:28:56.490
I forgot in my introduction to point out

01:28:56.490 --> 01:28:58.650
how meticulous his scholarship is

01:28:58.650 --> 01:29:00.090
and what a range it covers.

01:29:00.090 --> 01:29:02.370
But we've seen that today in abundance.

01:29:02.370 --> 01:29:03.630
So thank you very much.

01:29:03.630 --> 01:29:05.673
I think it's given everybody something.

01:29:07.200 --> 01:29:09.900
Just the discussions and our
thoughts about modern China

01:29:09.900 --> 01:29:11.550
and the relationships between

01:29:11.550 --> 01:29:14.490
the French singularity
and China, for example.

01:29:14.490 --> 01:29:16.950
Or the fact that everyone was
better informed about China

01:29:16.950 --> 01:29:19.830
in the mid 18th century are fascinating

01:29:19.830 --> 01:29:22.050
for us to think about
contemporary politics.

01:29:22.050 --> 01:29:24.818
So thank you very much
for such a superb talk.

01:29:24.818 --> 01:29:27.985
(audience applauding)

01:29:29.097 --> 01:29:31.847
(soothing music)

